export const pages = {"/botany/tree-biology": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Botany</p><p class=\"standfirst\">How trees capture energy, move water, grow, compartmentalize injury, and organize their major tissues and organs.</p></div>\n<main>\n<div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Photosynthesis and respiration</li><li>Leaves, stems, and roots</li><li>Primary and secondary growth</li><li>Water movement and mycorrhizae</li><li>CODIT and palms</li></ul></div>\n<h2>Energy: photosynthesis and respiration</h2><p><strong>Photosynthesis</strong> uses light energy to form glucose from water and carbon dioxide, releasing oxygen. Glucose can be used immediately, stored as starch, or converted into compounds such as cellulose, lipids, amino acids, and other carbohydrates.</p><p><strong>Respiration</strong> releases usable energy from carbohydrates using oxygen. Trees depend on the balance between photosynthesis and respiration to support growth, defense, transport, reproduction, and storage.</p><div class=\"pull\"><p>Photosynthesis can be restricted by shade, drought, temperature, chlorophyll content, soil oxygen, and nutrient deficiency.</p></div>\n<h2>Leaves, stems, and roots</h2><p>Leaves are major sites of photosynthesis and gas exchange. <strong>Transpiration</strong> is water-vapor loss, primarily through leaf stomata, and is a major driver of water movement through the tree.</p><p>Stems support the crown and contain conducting tissues. <strong>Cambium</strong> is meristematic tissue that produces phloem outward and xylem inward, increasing stem and root diameter. Sapwood contains functional xylem; heartwood is older inner wood.</p><p>Roots provide absorption, conduction, storage, gas exchange, and support. Fine absorbing roots depend on suitable oxygen, moisture, and soil conditions. <strong>Mycorrhizae</strong> are symbiotic associations between fungi and absorbing roots.</p>\n<h2>Primary and secondary growth</h2><p><strong>Primary growth</strong> increases length and occurs at meristematic growing points such as buds and root tips. <strong>Secondary growth</strong> increases girth through cambial activity. Earlywood and latewood reflect different portions of the annual growth cycle.</p><h2>Water uptake and osmosis</h2><p><strong>Osmosis</strong> is movement of water through a semipermeable membrane from higher water potential toward lower water potential. Root structure regulates the movement of water and dissolved materials into vascular tissues.</p>\n<h2>Compartmentalization</h2><p>Compartmentalization of decay in trees describes four walls that resist spread after injury. Wall 1 resists vertical spread, Wall 2 inward spread, Wall 3 lateral spread, and Wall 4 separates newly formed tissue from injured or decayed tissue. Wall 4 is the barrier zone formed after injury.</p><details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Check: Which growth process increases a stem's diameter?</summary><div><p>Secondary growth, produced through cambial activity.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Palms are different</h2><p>Palms are monocots. They do not have a vascular cambium like typical woody trees and do not undergo the same secondary growth. Their stems contain vascular bundles, and growth depends on a single primary apical meristem.</p>\n\n</main>", "/botany/section-quiz": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Botany</p><p class=\"standfirst\">A cumulative quiz drawing from the lessons in this section. Questions are shuffled on each attempt; answer all questions, then check your score and review the explanations.</p></div>\n<main><section class=\"activity section-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-toolbar\"><p id=\"quizProgress\"></p><button id=\"restartQuiz\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New attempt</button></div><div id=\"sectionQuiz\"></div><div class=\"actions\"><button id=\"gradeQuiz\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button></div><div id=\"quizResult\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></section></main>", "/botany/tree-identification": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Botany</p><p class=\"standfirst\">A practical framework for taxonomy and recognizing trees by foliage, bark, branching, cones, flowers, fruit, and other morphological traits.</p></div>\n<main>\n<div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Taxonomy</li><li>Scientific names</li><li>Conifer clues</li><li>Leaves and branching</li><li>Flowers and fruit</li></ul></div><h2>Taxonomy as an identification framework</h2><p>Taxonomy classifies organisms according to natural relationships. The hierarchy becomes increasingly specific from domain through kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, and species. Organisms grouped more closely generally share more morphological features.</p><h2>Scientific names</h2><p>A species name combines a <strong>genus</strong> with a <strong>specific epithet</strong>, such as <em>Pinus ponderosa</em>. Common names can vary, so scientific names provide a more consistent reference.</p><h2>Conifer clues</h2><p>Useful traits include foliage arrangement, attachment, bark, and cones. Fir (<em>Abies</em>) foliage is generally single, flat, and blunt, with upright cones. Spruce (<em>Picea</em>) foliage is generally single, stiff, and sharp, attached on woody pegs, with hanging cones. Pine (<em>Pinus</em>) needles are commonly grouped in fascicles and the cones are woody.</p><h2>Broadleaf identification</h2><p>Look at overall form and bark, then examine branch or leaf alignment. Leaves may be simple or compound. Useful leaf characters include shape, margin, tip, base, texture, and smell. Flowers may occur singly or in inflorescences, and fruits can be highly diagnostic.</p><div class=\"pull\"><p>Identification is strongest when several independent traits point to the same taxon rather than relying on one feature.</p></div>\n\n</main>", "/soil-and-nutrition/soil-analysis": "<div class=\"page-head\">\n\t<p class=\"standfirst\">\n\t\tPractice using the soil texture triangle and the pH scale.\n\t</p>\n</div>\n\n<main>\n\n\t<section class=\"activity\" aria-labelledby=\"triangle-heading\">\n\t\t<h2 id=\"triangle-heading\">Using the soil texture triangle</h2>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tThe soil texture triangle plots the percentages of sand, silt, and clay in a soil\n\t\t\tsample to determine its texture class. Given any two of the three percentages, the\n\t\t\tthird can be found by subtracting from 100%, and the texture class can be read from\n\t\t\twhere those values intersect on the triangle.\n\t\t</p>\n\t\t<div id=\"soil-triangle-root\"></div>\n\t\t<h3>Practice questions</h3>\n\t\t<p>Use the triangle above to answer these.</p>\n\t\t<div id=\"textureQuestions\" class=\"question-list\"></div>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"checkTextureButton\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"resetTextureButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New Questions</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div id=\"textureFeedback\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\t</section>\n\n\t<section class=\"activity\" aria-labelledby=\"ph-heading\">\n\t\t<h2 id=\"ph-heading\">Using the pH scale</h2>\n\t\t<p>The pH scale runs from 0 to 14, with plants often preferring a pH between 6.0 and 6.5.</p>\n\t\t<div id=\"phQuestions\" class=\"question-list\"></div>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"checkPhButton\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"resetPhButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New Questions</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div id=\"phFeedback\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\t</section>\n\n\t\n\n</main>", "/soil-and-nutrition/tree-nutrition": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Soils &amp; Nutrition</p><p class=\"standfirst\">A nutrient can be present in the soil and still be unavailable to a tree. Understanding nutrition means looking beyond the amount of an element to the soil and plant processes that control uptake.</p></div>\n<main>\n  <div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Essential elements</li><li>Macro- and micronutrients</li><li>Nutrient availability</li><li>Deficiency symptoms</li><li>Soil and foliar testing</li></ul></div>\n  <h2>Essential elements</h2>\n  <p>Nutrients participate in tree metabolism and other plant processes. The course notes that plants require approximately <strong>16 to 19 essential elements</strong>. Carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen are obtained from the atmosphere, while other elements can be obtained through uptake from soil, internal cycling, and atmospheric deposition such as rainfall and dust.</p>\n  <h3>Macro- and micronutrients</h3>\n  <p><strong>Macronutrients</strong> are required in relatively large quantities. The primary macronutrients are nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium; secondary macronutrients include sulfur, magnesium, and calcium. <strong>Micronutrients</strong> are required in smaller quantities, but can still limit growth.</p>\n\n  <h2>Nutrient availability</h2>\n  <p>The presence of a nutrient in a soil analysis does not guarantee that a tree can take it up. The course identifies several causes of nutrient problems, including low soil organic matter, acidic or alkaline soil, altered or compacted soil, waterlogging, wilt diseases, cankers, borers, volatilization, and leaching—especially in sandy soils with low organic matter.</p>\n  <div class=\"pull\"><p>Think of nutrient status as an interaction among the nutrient supply, soil conditions, root function, and the tree's ability to transport and use what it absorbs.</p></div>\n\n  <h2>Recognizing deficiencies</h2>\n  <p>General nutrient-deficiency symptoms can include reduced growth, small size, and chlorosis. The lecture uses nitrogen deficiency as an example: chlorosis is observed initially on older leaves and, with time, may appear on both young and old leaves.</p>\n  <details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Check: A soil test reports plenty of a nutrient, but the tree appears deficient. Is that possible?</summary><div><p>Yes. Soil pH, compaction, waterlogging, root or vascular problems, and other factors can interfere with availability, uptake, or transport even when a nutrient is present in the soil.</p></div></details>\n\n  <h2>Testing</h2>\n  <p>A <strong>soil test</strong> can provide information about plant-available nutrients, soil organic matter, soil pH, and cation exchange capacity. A <strong>foliar test</strong> measures nutrient content in foliage. Used together, these tests can help distinguish nutrient supply in the soil from nutrient status in the plant.</p>\n  <p>Testing is especially important before assuming that fertilization is the appropriate response to poor growth or chlorosis.</p>\n\n  <h2>From diagnosis to treatment</h2>\n  <p>Fertilizers can increase nutrient supply and address deficiencies, but the course emphasizes timing with root growth and the need for sufficient soil moisture. Fertilizer choice and application should therefore follow diagnosis rather than replace it.</p>\n  <p><a href=\"/soil-and-nutrition/tree-fertilization/\">Continue to Tree Fertilization →</a></p>\n  \n</main>", "/soil-and-nutrition/water-management": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Soils &amp; Nutrition</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Trees need enough water to maintain physiological processes, but too much water can be just as damaging as too little. Good water management starts with the plant, the soil, and the weather.</p></div>\n<main>\n  <div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Water demand and evapotranspiration</li><li>Drought and flooding</li><li>Soil moisture reservoir</li><li>Irrigation methods</li><li>Water quality and conservation</li><li>Mulch and drainage</li></ul></div>\n  <h2>How much water does a tree need?</h2>\n  <p>Water needs vary with the <strong>plant</strong>—including species, size, and growth rate—and with <strong>climate</strong>, including air temperature, humidity, light, wind, and season. Evapotranspiration combines water lost through plant transpiration and evaporation from the soil.</p>\n  <p>If transpiration exceeds precipitation and soil moisture reaches the wilting point, irrigation may be necessary.</p>\n\n  <h2>Drought</h2>\n  <p>Drought conditions provide insufficient water for transpiration and photosynthesis and cause loss of turgidity. Symptoms described in the course include marginal leaf scorch, leaf drop, twig dieback, temporary afternoon wilting, root loss, and—in severe cases—plant death.</p>\n  <p>Temporary wilting can occur on hot summer afternoons when transpiration exceeds the tree's ability to take up water, with recovery overnight. Adaptations to arid conditions can include deep spreading roots, leaf shedding or temporary dormancy, smaller leaves, thicker cuticles, and sunken stomata.</p>\n\n  <h2>Flooding</h2>\n  <p>Flooded soils restrict air and gas exchange. Flooding can also leach essential nutrients, contribute to mineral toxicity, and produce gleyed grey or purple-grey soils. Tree symptoms can include leaf yellowing, defoliation, crown dieback, root death from oxygen deprivation, fermentation, rot, and disease. Flooding can also increase risk of tree failure.</p>\n  <p>Some plants are adapted to wet conditions through specialized root and stem tissues that transport oxygen or through shallow rooting that improves access to oxygen near the surface.</p>\n  <details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Diagnostic caution: drought or something else?</summary><div><p>Symptoms associated with water stress can have lookalikes. The course identifies girdling roots, trunk injury or cankers, vascular wilt disease, insects affecting xylem, and lightning damage as problems that can disrupt water uptake or transport.</p></div></details>\n\n  <h2>The soil moisture reservoir</h2>\n  <p>The <strong>soil moisture reservoir</strong> is the volume of soil occupied by roots together with the water-holding capacity of that soil. It is lower in sandy soils than in clay soils and lower for plants with small root systems.</p>\n  <p>Irrigation needs therefore depend on tree species and size, the amount of water stored in the root-zone soil, and environmental conditions.</p>\n\n  <h2>Irrigation</h2>\n  <h3>Newly planted trees</h3>\n  <p>Newly planted trees deserve particular attention because roots may have been lost or damaged during harvesting and planting, and the remaining root system is initially concentrated within the root ball. The course recommends irrigating around the periphery of the root ball, then reducing or eliminating irrigation frequency as roots grow and the tree establishes.</p>\n  <h3>Common systems</h3>\n  <table class=\"key-table\"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Characteristics and use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n    <tr><td>Spray</td><td>Small droplets and a slow application rate; works best on level sites in calm weather and should be scheduled to infiltration rate and weather.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Bubbler</td><td>Stream or umbrella pattern with moderate-to-fast application; used for newly planted trees, often just outside the root ball with a berm to retain water.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Drip</td><td>Slow delivery from hose or tubing; place close to roots, schedule to plant and soil needs, and inspect for leaks or clogged emitters.</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Other methods</td><td>High-pressure water injection, soaker hoses, basin irrigation, and water bags.</td></tr>\n  </tbody></table>\n  <div class=\"pull\"><p>Irrigation should not be applied faster than water can infiltrate. Avoid puddling, runoff, and erosion; shallow watering can encourage surface rooting.</p></div>\n\n  <h2>Water quality and conservation</h2>\n  <p>Water-quality concerns include salt or nutrient accumulation, disease such as Phytophthora, clogged irrigation nozzles, and pollutants. The course recommends soil salinity testing where appropriate.</p>\n  <p>Conservation approaches include using only the irrigation needed, xeriscaping with drought-resistant landscapes, grouping plants into hydrozones according to water needs, and monitoring soil moisture before and after irrigation.</p>\n\n  <h2>Mulch and drainage</h2>\n  <p>Mulch can reduce evaporation, improve soil conditions, reduce weed competition, moderate summer soil temperature, reduce mechanical injury, and support soil biological processes. The lecture recommends a mulch depth of approximately <strong>2–4 inches</strong>.</p>\n  <p>Drain tiles can help address persistent flooding by removing gravitational water, but the course emphasizes that they are <strong>not</strong> a fix for heavy clay soils or improper irrigation practices.</p>\n  <details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Check: Why might drainage tiles fail to solve a chronically wet site?</summary><div><p>They remove gravitational water. A heavy clay soil or excessive irrigation can still leave the soil too wet even after gravitational water has drained.</p></div></details>\n  \n</main>", "/soil-and-nutrition/soil-science": "<div class=\"page-head\">\n  <p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Soils &amp; Nutrition</p>\n  <p class=\"standfirst\">Soil is more than a rooting medium. Its physical structure, chemistry, biology, and water relationships determine how effectively a tree can obtain oxygen, water, and mineral elements.</p>\n</div>\n<main>\n  <div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Soil formation and horizons</li><li>Texture, structure, porosity, and bulk density</li><li>pH, buffering, and cation exchange</li><li>Soil biology and root growth</li><li>Water movement</li><li>Compaction and urban soils</li></ul></div>\n\n  <h2>How soils form</h2>\n  <p>Five broad factors influence soil formation: <strong>climate, organisms, relief or topography, parent material, and time</strong>. Climate includes precipitation and temperature; organisms include vegetation, microbes, soil animals, and people; relief includes slope, aspect, and elevation; parent material is the unconsolidated material in which soil develops; and time allows these processes to act.</p>\n  <div class=\"concept-grid\">\n    <div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Climate</h3><p>Precipitation and temperature influence weathering and biological activity.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Organisms</h3><p>Plants, microbes, soil animals, and humans all participate in soil development.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Relief</h3><p>Slope, aspect, and elevation alter drainage, exposure, and local conditions.</p></div>\n    <div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Parent material + time</h3><p>Soil develops from parent material as weathering and biological processes operate over time.</p></div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h3>Soil horizons</h3>\n  <p>A soil profile can contain several distinct horizons. The <strong>O horizon</strong> is an organic surface layer containing materials such as leaves, twigs, bark, and organisms. The <strong>A horizon</strong> is rich in soil organic matter and contains many absorbing roots. An <strong>E horizon</strong>, where present, is a zone of mineral weathering and eluviation and contains less organic matter than the A horizon. The <strong>B horizon</strong> is a zone of accumulation. The <strong>C horizon</strong> is partially weathered parent material above unweathered material or bedrock.</p>\n  <details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Check: Why is the A horizon especially important to arborists?</summary><div><p>It contains much of the tree's absorbing root system and is rich in soil organic matter, making conditions near the soil surface especially important for tree health.</p></div></details>\n\n  <h2>Soil physics</h2>\n  <h3>Organic matter</h3>\n  <p>Soil organic matter consists of dead plant and animal material. It changes with moisture, helps bind mineral particles together, and can be increased through practices such as adding mulch or compost.</p>\n\n  <h3>Texture</h3>\n  <p><strong>Soil texture</strong> describes the relative proportions of sand, silt, and clay. Sand particles are the largest, clay particles are the smallest, and the balance among the three strongly affects water holding, aeration, compaction, nutrient supply, and other soil behaviour.</p>\n  <table class=\"key-table\"><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Sand</th><th>Silt</th><th>Clay</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n    <tr><td>Water holding</td><td>Low</td><td>Medium–high</td><td>High</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Aeration</td><td>High</td><td>Medium</td><td>Poor</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Organic matter decomposition</td><td>Fast</td><td>Medium</td><td>Slow</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Compactability</td><td>Low</td><td>Medium</td><td>High</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>Nutrient supplying</td><td>Poor</td><td>Medium–high</td><td>High</td></tr>\n  </tbody></table>\n  <p><a href=\"/soil-and-nutrition/soil-analysis/\">Practice reading the soil texture triangle →</a></p>\n\n  <h3>Structure, aggregates, and pore space</h3>\n  <p>Primary soil particles can group into <strong>aggregates</strong>, which give soil structure. Flocculation groups particles through interparticle forces, while cementation stabilizes those groups using agents such as organic matter and clays.</p>\n  <p>Pore space is just as important as the solid particles. <strong>Macropores</strong> are relatively large spaces that promote aeration and drainage but are too large to retain much water against gravity. <strong>Micropores</strong> are smaller and retain water between precipitation events. Coarse soils tend to be dominated by macropores; fine soils contain more micropores.</p>\n  <p><strong>Bulk density</strong> is the weight of dried soil per unit volume. High bulk density is associated with low porosity. This relationship is particularly important when assessing compacted urban soils.</p>\n\n  <h2>Soil chemistry</h2>\n  <h3>pH and buffering</h3>\n  <p>Soil pH measures acidity or alkalinity on a scale from 0 to 14. The scale is logarithmic: a pH of 5 is ten times more acidic than a pH of 6. The course material identifies approximately 6.0–6.5 as favourable for plants.</p>\n  <p><strong>Buffering capacity</strong> is a soil's resistance to pH change. Soils high in clay and soil organic matter generally have greater buffering capacity. Sulfur can lower pH and lime can raise it, although their effects may be limited spatially and temporally.</p>\n  <p><a href=\"/soil-and-nutrition/soil-analysis/#ph-heading\">Practice using the pH scale →</a></p>\n\n  <h3>Cations and cation exchange capacity</h3>\n  <p>Anions carry a negative charge and cations carry a positive charge. <strong>Cation exchange capacity (CEC)</strong> describes a soil's ability to attract, retain, and exchange positively charged cations. Clay and organic matter have high negative charge density, so clay-rich and organic soils tend to have higher CEC.</p>\n  <div class=\"pull\"><p>A higher CEC is associated with greater soil buffering, greater retention of nutrients in available forms, and greater retention of some contaminants.</p></div>\n  <p>Saline soils contain excessive salts. Sodic soils are high in sodium and may develop crusting, high pH, nutrient deficiencies or imbalances, and poor structure.</p>\n\n  <h2>Soil biology and roots</h2>\n  <p>Soil is biologically active. The course highlights mycorrhizal fungi and soil fauna, with fauna grouped as micro-, meso-, and macrofauna. Roots grow where conditions are favourable and require space, organic materials, mineral elements, water, and gas exchange. Many absorbing roots occur close to the soil surface.</p>\n\n  <h2>Water in soil</h2>\n  <p>Water movement depends on texture and the arrangement of soil layers. In a <strong>sand-over-clay</strong> profile, water can accumulate in the sand because it percolates slowly into the clay. In a <strong>clay-over-sand</strong> profile, a capillary barrier can cause water to accumulate in the clay until the upper layer becomes saturated enough for water to move into the sand.</p>\n  <details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Check: Why can adding a contrasting soil layer create drainage problems?</summary><div><p>Water does not necessarily move freely across a sharp textural boundary. Differences in pore size and percolation can cause water to accumulate above the boundary.</p></div></details>\n\n  <h2>Compaction and urban soils</h2>\n  <p>Compaction reduces total pore space and can make it difficult for trees to obtain water, oxygen, and mineral elements. Management options presented in the course include air excavation, radial trenching, and tillage.</p>\n  <p>Urban soils may also have abrupt textural differences, reduced or missing O horizons, altered biological activity, and greater fluctuations in surface soil temperature. Structural soils are engineered to meet building requirements while allowing root growth. Structural cells instead carry structural loads while maintaining spaces for uncompacted soil, though they are costly.</p>\n  \n</main>", "/soil-and-nutrition/section-quiz": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Soils &amp; Nutrition</p><p class=\"standfirst\">A cumulative quiz drawing from the lessons in this section. Questions are shuffled on each attempt; answer all questions, then check your score and review the explanations.</p></div>\n<main><section class=\"activity section-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-toolbar\"><p id=\"quizProgress\"></p><button id=\"restartQuiz\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New attempt</button></div><div id=\"sectionQuiz\"></div><div class=\"actions\"><button id=\"gradeQuiz\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button></div><div id=\"quizResult\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></section></main>", "/soil-and-nutrition/tree-fertilization": "<div class=\"page-head\">\n\t<p class=\"standfirst\">\n\t\tIn a complete fertilizer, the three primary nutrients are nitrogen (N), phosphorus (P),\n\t\tand potassium (K). The N-P-K label, also called the guaranteed analysis, shows the\n\t\tpercentage of nutrients in the product by weight.\n\t</p>\n\t<p class=\"standfirst\">\n\t\tThis activity helps you practice the math used in determining fertilizer application amounts.\n\t</p>\n</div>\n\n<main>\n\n\t<p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Soils &amp; Nutrition</p>\n\t<div class=\"lesson-nav\">\n\t\t<strong>On this page</strong>\n\t\t<ul>\n\t\t\t<li>When fertilization may be appropriate</li>\n\t\t\t<li>Application methods and trade-offs</li>\n\t\t\t<li>Fertilizer burn and testing</li>\n\t\t\t<li>Understanding N-P-K labels</li>\n\t\t\t<li>Fertilizer calculations and practice</li>\n\t\t</ul>\n\t</div>\n\n\t<h2>Using fertilizer as a treatment</h2>\n\t<p>\n\t\tFertilizers can increase nutrient supply and may be used to address a diagnosed deficiency.\n\t\tThe course material emphasizes that application should be timed with root growth and that\n\t\tsufficient soil moisture is needed. Fertilization is therefore a treatment decision, not a\n\t\tsubstitute for diagnosing why a tree is performing poorly.\n\t</p>\n\t<p>\n\t\tSoil analysis can provide information about plant-available nutrients, organic matter, pH,\n\t\tand cation exchange capacity. Foliar analysis measures nutrient content in foliage. These\n\t\tresults, together with site conditions, can help determine whether added nutrients are likely\n\t\tto address the problem.\n\t</p>\n\n\t<h2>Application methods</h2>\n\t<table class=\"key-table\">\n\t\t<thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Course notes</th></tr></thead>\n\t\t<tbody>\n\t\t\t<tr><td>Surface application</td><td>Dry fertilizer may be spread and liquid fertilizer sprayed on the soil surface. It is easy and low-cost, but nutrients can run off or volatilize, and trees may compete with turfgrass.</td></tr>\n\t\t\t<tr><td>Subsurface application</td><td>Dry fertilizer can be drilled or liquid fertilizer injected into the root zone, improving distribution and uptake.</td></tr>\n\t\t\t<tr><td>Foliar spray</td><td>Applied to leaves for rapid plant use, but the effect is temporary and material may drip through the canopy.</td></tr>\n\t\t\t<tr><td>Injection or implant</td><td>Material is introduced through drilled holes into the tree. The course limits this use to micronutrients, cautions against use on drought-stressed trees, and notes that drilling wounds the tree.</td></tr>\n\t\t</tbody>\n\t</table>\n\n\t<h2>Before applying fertilizer</h2>\n\t<p>\n\t\tMisuse can cause <strong>fertilizer burn</strong>. The lecture recommends considering slow-release\n\t\tfertilizers and using soil—and where appropriate foliar—analysis to identify deficiencies.\n\t\tSoil pH and drought conditions should also be considered before treatment.\n\t</p>\n\t<details class=\"knowledge-check\">\n\t\t<summary>Check: Why should chlorosis not automatically trigger fertilization?</summary>\n\t\t<div><p>Because nutrient symptoms can result from problems with availability or uptake, including soil pH, drought, compaction, waterlogging, and root or vascular problems. Testing and site diagnosis should come before treatment.</p></div>\n\t</details>\n\n\t<details class=\"explainer\">\n\t\t<summary>Nerd-to-know note on N-P-K labels</summary>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tOn the label, nitrogen is listed as actual N, but phosphorus and potassium are usually\n\t\t\treported as available phosphoric acid (P<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>) and soluble potash\n\t\t\t(K<sub>2</sub>O). This means the label tells us how much of each labeled nutrient is in\n\t\t\tthe fertilizer, not the exact elemental amount of phosphorus or potassium.\n\t\t</p>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tFor example, a guaranteed analysis of 30-15-10 means that the bag contains 30% N,\n\t\t\t15% P<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>, and 10% K<sub>2</sub>O.\n\t\t</p>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t</p>\n\t</details>\n\n\t<details class=\"explainer\">\n\t\t<summary>How to find the amount of each nutrient</summary>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tWe can calculate how many pounds of each nutrient are in the bag by multiplying the\n\t\t\ttotal bag weight by each percentage and dividing by 100.\n\t\t</p>\n\n\t\t<p class=\"formula\">Nutrient weight = Total bag weight &times; Nutrient percentage &divide; 100</p>\n\n\t\t<div class=\"example\">\n\t\t\t<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\t\t\t<p id=\"workedExampleIntro\">\n\t\t\t\tA 20 lb bag of complete fertilizer with a guaranteed analysis of 10&ndash;6&ndash;3\n\t\t\t\tcontains 10% nitrogen, 6% available phosphate, and 3% soluble potash. The amount of\n\t\t\t\teach nutrient in the bag is calculated as follows:\n\t\t\t</p>\n\t\t\t<ul>\n\t\t\t\t<li id=\"workedExampleN\">Nitrogen: 20 &times; 10 &divide; 100 = <strong>2.0 lb</strong></li>\n\t\t\t\t<li id=\"workedExampleP\">Available phosphate: 20 &times; 6 &divide; 100 = <strong>1.2 lb</strong></li>\n\t\t\t\t<li id=\"workedExampleK\">Soluble potash: 20 &times; 3 &divide; 100 = <strong>0.6 lb</strong></li>\n\t\t\t</ul>\n\t\t</div>\n\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tIn other words, the numbers on the bag tell you the percentage of each nutrient by\n\t\t\tmass, which can be converted into the actual weight of nutrient in the product.\n\t\t</p>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tYou can also simplify the calculation by using the decimal form of the percentage\n\t\t\tinstead of dividing by 100. Since 10% is the same as 0.10, you can multiply the bag\n\t\t\tweight directly by 0.10 to get the nutrient mass. This is a faster way to do the\n\t\t\tsame calculation.\n\t\t</p>\n\n\t\t<div class=\"example\">\n\t\t\t<h3>Worked example</h3>\n\t\t\t<p id=\"workedExampleIntro2\">\n\t\t\t\tA 10 lb bag of complete fertilizer with a guaranteed analysis of 20-10-5 contains\n\t\t\t\tthe following amounts of nutrients:\n\t\t\t</p>\n\t\t\t<ul>\n\t\t\t\t<li id=\"workedExampleN2\">Nitrogen: 10 &times; 0.20 = <strong>2.0 lb</strong></li>\n\t\t\t\t<li id=\"workedExampleP2\">Available phosphate: 10 &times; 0.10 = <strong>1.0 lb</strong></li>\n\t\t\t\t<li id=\"workedExampleK2\">Soluble potash: 10 &times; 0.05 = <strong>0.5 lb</strong></li>\n\t\t\t</ul>\n\t\t</div>\n\t</details>\n\n\t<section class=\"activity\" aria-labelledby=\"label-heading\">\n\t\t<div class=\"activity-head\">\n\t\t\t<h2 id=\"label-heading\">Using the bag&rsquo;s guaranteed analysis</h2>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"unit-toggle\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Units for amount questions\">\n\t\t\t\t<span id=\"unitLabelLb\" class=\"unit-toggle-label\">lb</span>\n\t\t\t\t<button type=\"button\" id=\"unitToggle\" class=\"switch\" role=\"switch\" aria-checked=\"false\" aria-label=\"Toggle amount questions between pounds and kilograms\"></button>\n\t\t\t\t<span id=\"unitLabelKg\" class=\"unit-toggle-label\">kg</span>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tThis 50 lb (22.7 kg) bag of complete fertilizer includes a guaranteed analysis. Use\n\t\t\tthe weight and guaranteed analysis to answer the three questions.\n\t\t</p>\n\t\t<div class=\"label-quiz\">\n\t\t\t<div class=\"bag-svg-wrap\"><img id=\"bagImage\" src=\"\" alt=\"\"></div>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"label-questions\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"question\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<label id=\"q1Label\" for=\"q1Answer\"></label>\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"input-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<input id=\"q1Answer\" class=\"answer-input\" type=\"number\" step=\"1\" inputmode=\"decimal\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<span id=\"q1Suffix\"></span>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"question\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<label id=\"q2Label\" for=\"q2Answer\"></label>\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"input-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<input id=\"q2Answer\" class=\"answer-input\" type=\"number\" step=\"0.1\" inputmode=\"decimal\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<span id=\"q2Suffix\"></span>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"question\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<label id=\"q3Label\" for=\"q3Answer\"></label>\n\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"input-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<input id=\"q3Answer\" class=\"answer-input\" type=\"number\" step=\"0.1\" inputmode=\"decimal\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<span id=\"q3Suffix\"></span>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"checkLabelButton\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"resetLabelButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New Bag</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div id=\"labelFeedback\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\t</section>\n\n\t<section class=\"activity\" aria-labelledby=\"practice-heading\">\n\t\t<div class=\"activity-head\">\n\t\t\t<h2 id=\"practice-heading\">Complete the table</h2>\n\t\t\t<div class=\"unit-toggle\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Units for the practice table\">\n\t\t\t\t<span id=\"practiceUnitLabelLb\" class=\"unit-toggle-label\">lb</span>\n\t\t\t\t<button type=\"button\" id=\"practiceUnitToggle\" class=\"switch\" role=\"switch\" aria-checked=\"false\" aria-label=\"Toggle practice table units between kilograms and pounds\"></button>\n\t\t\t\t<span id=\"practiceUnitLabelKg\" class=\"unit-toggle-label\">kg</span>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<p id=\"practiceUnitHint\">Using the bag weight and guaranteed analysis, enter the amount of nitrogen, available phosphate, and soluble potash in kilograms. Decimals are allowed.\n\nThe first row is completed as an example.</p>\n\t\t<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n\t\t\t<table>\n\t\t\t\t<thead><tr><th id=\"colWeightHeader\">Bag weight</th><th>Guaranteed analysis</th><th id=\"colNHeader\">kg of N</th><th id=\"colPHeader\">kg of P</th><th id=\"colKHeader\">kg of K</th></tr></thead>\n\t\t\t\t<tbody id=\"exampleRowBody\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<tr class=\"example-row\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<td id=\"exampleWeightCell\">20 kg</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<td id=\"exampleAnalysisCell\">10&ndash;6&ndash;3</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<td id=\"exampleNCell\">2.0</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<td id=\"examplePCell\">1.2</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<td id=\"exampleKCell\">0.6</td>\n\t\t\t\t\t</tr>\n\t\t\t\t</tbody>\n\t\t\t\t<tbody id=\"practiceRows\"></tbody>\n\t\t\t</table>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"checkButton\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"hintButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">Show a Hint</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"resetButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">Reset</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div id=\"feedback\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\t\t<div id=\"hint\" class=\"hint\" hidden></div>\n\t</section>\n\n\t<section class=\"activity\" aria-labelledby=\"bags-heading\">\n\t\t<h2 id=\"bags-heading\">Challenge: how many bags are needed?</h2>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tCalculate how many bags of complete fertilizer are needed to supply the required amount\n\t\t\tof nutrients. Round up to the nearest integer, as only full bags can be purchased.\n\t\t</p>\n\t\t<div id=\"bagQuestions\" class=\"question-list\"></div>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"checkBagsButton\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Challenge</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"resetBagsButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">Reset Challenge</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div id=\"bagFeedback\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\t</section>\n\n\t\n\n</main>", "/arboriculture-operations/tree-risk-and-support": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Arboriculture Operations</p><p class=\"standfirst\">How tree structure, loads, defects, targets, consequences, mitigation, support systems, and lightning protection fit into tree risk management.</p></div>\n<main>\n<div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Loads and response growth</li><li>Decay and defects</li><li>Risk assessment</li><li>Risk management</li><li>Support systems</li></ul></div><h2>Loads, strength, and response growth</h2><p>Trees respond to their environment by adding wood where mechanical strain occurs. <strong>Response growth</strong> includes reaction wood, flexure wood, and woundwood. Loads combine forces; when a load exceeds the strength of a tree part, failure occurs. Gravity is a common static force, while wind produces dynamic loading.</p><h2>Decay and defects</h2><p>Decay can reduce wood strength. The material distinguishes white rot, brown rot, and soft rot and emphasizes inspection for structural indicators such as poor taper, root failure, codominant branches, cankers or wounds, cracks, decay indicators, dead branches, human damage, changes in lean, and root or soil movement.</p><h2>Risk combines likelihood and consequences</h2><p>Tree risk assessment considers a tree or tree part, a potential target, the likelihood of failure, the likelihood of impact, and the consequences if impact occurs. Occupancy, target zone, time frame, part size, and distance influence the assessment.</p><p>The three assessment levels presented are <strong>limited visual</strong> for broad screening, <strong>basic</strong> for detailed visual inspection of a tree and site, and <strong>advanced</strong> for questions requiring specialized equipment, data, analysis, or expertise.</p><h2>Management and mitigation</h2><p>All trees pose some risk and also provide benefits. Management involves deciding what level of risk is acceptable and selecting mitigation. Mitigation can be tree-based—such as pruning or health improvement—or target-based. Residual risk remains after treatment.</p><h2>Support systems</h2><p>Support options include bracing, static cabling, dynamic cabling, props, and guying. Dynamic systems permit movement and can be non-invasive but require inspection and maintenance. Static systems and bracing can provide durable support but commonly require drilling. Any support system changes how a tree moves and must be installed and inspected according to the system manufacturer's recommendations.</p><h2>Lightning protection</h2><p>Lightning can split stems, damage tissues, and side-flash to nearby structures. Protection systems are intended to provide a preferred conductive path toward ground and require specialized design, installation, and inspection.</p>\n\n</main>", "/arboriculture-operations/worker-safety-and-climbing": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Arboriculture Operations</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Core jobsite safety concepts, communication, work zones, electrical awareness, climbing terminology, rope systems, and emergency response.</p></div>\n<main>\n<div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>PPE and job briefings</li><li>Work zones and communication</li><li>Electrical and equipment hazards</li><li>Climbing and rope systems</li><li>Emergency response</li></ul></div><h2>Personal protective equipment</h2><p>Core PPE includes head, eye, and hearing protection, with task-specific additions such as cut-resistant leg protection for chainsaw work and manufacturer- or regulation-directed protection for pesticide work. Clothing should permit movement without being loose enough to snag.</p><h2>Job briefings and work zones</h2><p>A job briefing coordinates who will do what, identifies hazards and controls, identifies required PPE, and establishes the communication system. A work plan should include emergency contacts, the nearest medical facility, and emergency transportation information.</p><p>The <strong>work zone</strong> is the overall area affected by the job. A <strong>drop or landing zone</strong> is where cut material is expected to fall or be lowered and must be clear before cutting.</p><h2>Electrical, fuel, and equipment hazards</h2><p>Treat electrical conductors as potentially carrying fatal voltage. Workers must be trained before using equipment or performing assigned tasks. Fuel spills should be cleaned before engines are started, smoking is prohibited around flammable liquids, and fuel must be stored in approved containers away from ropes and equipment.</p><h2>Climbing terminology</h2><p><strong>Tensile strength</strong> is the breaking strength of new rope or hardware. <strong>Working-load limit</strong> is the highest intended load. A <strong>design factor</strong> relates breaking strength to intended working load. A knot is tied in one rope, a hitch secures a rope to an object or rope, and a bend joins rope ends.</p><p>In a <strong>moving rope system (MRS)</strong>, the climbing line moves through the tie-in point as the climber moves. In a <strong>stationary rope system (SRS)</strong>, the climber moves along a stationary line.</p><h2>Emergency response</h2><p>Assess site hazards and injuries, summon emergency medical services for emergencies, use first-aid PPE, and manage contaminated materials appropriately. Severe bleeding requires rapid control; the source material distinguishes direct pressure from tourniquet use depending on severity.</p><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Safety note:</strong> This study page summarizes course concepts. Actual tree work requires current training, applicable regulations, manufacturer instructions, and job-specific procedures.</div>\n\n</main>", "/arboriculture-operations/section-quiz": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Arboriculture Operations</p><p class=\"standfirst\">A cumulative quiz drawing from the lessons in this section. Questions are shuffled on each attempt; answer all questions, then check your score and review the explanations.</p></div>\n<main><section class=\"activity section-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-toolbar\"><p id=\"quizProgress\"></p><button id=\"restartQuiz\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New attempt</button></div><div id=\"sectionQuiz\"></div><div class=\"actions\"><button id=\"gradeQuiz\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button></div><div id=\"quizResult\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></section></main>", "/arboriculture-operations/pruning": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Arboriculture Operations</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Effective pruning starts with an objective, respects tree biology, and uses cuts that achieve the objective while limiting unnecessary injury.</p></div>\n<main><div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Objectives and specifications</li><li>Pruning biology</li><li>Timing, dose, and technique</li><li>Types of pruning cuts</li><li>Training and crown management</li><li>Pruning systems and special cases</li></ul></div>\n<h2>Start with an objective</h2><p>Pruning specifications can include <strong>clean</strong> (remove dead, diseased, or broken branches), <strong>thin</strong> (reduce live-branch density), <strong>raise</strong> (provide vertical clearance), and <strong>reduce</strong> (decrease height or spread). Broader objectives include improving structure, reducing risk, providing clearance, managing crown size, reducing crown density, and restoring structure.</p>\n<div class=\"pull\"><p>Pruning is not automatically beneficial. It can reduce wildlife habitat and photosynthetic capacity, create sapwood dysfunction and wounds requiring compartmentalization, and—when poorly performed—increase likelihood of failure.</p></div>\n<h2>Pruning biology</h2><p>Trees do not heal damaged wood; they <strong>seal</strong> and compartmentalize it. Large cuts take longer to seal. Correct branch removal respects the <strong>branch collar</strong>, <strong>branch bark ridge</strong>, and branch protection zone. Flush cuts remove protective tissues and are not appropriate.</p>\n<h2>Timing and dose</h2><p>Remove only what is necessary to meet the objective. Tolerance varies with age, structure, health, weather, and site; young trees generally tolerate more pruning than old trees. The course notes that no more than one-quarter of mature-tree foliage should be removed under the referenced guidance.</p><p>Pruning before bud swell can maximize subsequent growth and support compartmentalization; pruning after bud swell can minimize growth because resources have just been used for new growth. Dead, diseased, and broken branches can be removed in any season. Avoid pruning during drought, and consider pest transmission periods and local pruning restrictions.</p>\n<h3>Flowering and fruiting trees</h3><p>Current-season bloomers can be pruned in winter before leaf emergence or in summer after bloom. Last-season bloomers are pruned just after bloom to preserve the next season's flowers. Dormant pruning can emphasize structure; pruning after bloom can reduce fruit production.</p>\n<h2>Technique</h2><p>Use sharp, appropriately sized tools; bypass blades are preferred over anvil-type tools. The course advises against pruning while wearing climbing spurs and recommends sterilizing tools between cuts with a non-phytotoxic material or liquid. Use the three-cut method where needed to prevent tearing or splitting.</p>\n<h2>Types of pruning cuts</h2><table class=\"key-table\"><thead><tr><th>Cut</th><th>Purpose / definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Branch removal</strong></td><td>Removes the smaller of two stems or branches at a union; made outside the branch collar and branch bark ridge.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reduction</strong></td><td>Removes the larger of two branches at a union and directs growth to a remaining lateral; the course specifies a lateral at least one-third the diameter of the removed branch.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Heading</strong></td><td>Removes a stem to an internodal point or to a branch less than one-third the diameter of the removed branch; generally ill-advised on mature trees.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Shearing</strong></td><td>Stimulates dormant and adventitious buds; useful in topiary or hedging but does not produce a natural crown form.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Check: A large limb must be shortened while retaining a suitable lateral branch. Reduction or heading cut?</summary><div><p>A reduction cut, provided the retained lateral is appropriately sized. Heading cuts terminate a branch at an internode or at an undersized lateral and are generally inappropriate for mature-tree crown reduction.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Structure, clearance, and crown management</h2><p>Structural pruning can remove dead, diseased, broken, or weakly attached branches, address included bark and overextended branches, and promote a strong leader. Young-tree training establishes a dominant leader, lowest permanent branches, well-spaced scaffold branches, and subordinate temporary branches.</p><p>Clearance pruning may address infrastructure, sight lines, activities, other plants, or people. Directional pruning can direct growth away from utilities. Crown elevation removes lower branches, but over-elevation can increase likelihood of failure.</p><p>Size management uses reduction cuts (and less commonly heading cuts). Density reduction increases light or wind penetration and promotes interior foliage; <strong>lion tailing</strong>—removing lower and interior foliage—is malpractice.</p>\n<h2>Pollarding is not topping</h2><p><strong>Pollarding</strong> deliberately creates pollard heads, allows sprouts to grow from those heads, and repeatedly removes sprouts back to the same heads without damaging them. <strong>Topping</strong> removes a large portion of the crown with large heading cuts. The course links topping with difficult compartmentalization, weak new attachments, rapid regrowth, increased failure potential, and possible tree death.</p>\n<h2>Special cases</h2><p><strong>Palms:</strong> remove fronds, flowers, fruit, or loose petioles when they create dangerous conditions; generally do not remove green fronds, and avoid damaging living tissue. Palm wounds do not close over.</p><p><strong>Conifers:</strong> remove dead, dying, and diseased branches and, where appropriate, restore a dominant leader; generally do not pollard or reduce/head lateral branches.</p><p><strong>Shrubs:</strong> common objectives include height reduction, rejuvenation, and aesthetics. Shearing is appropriate only for tolerant plants and creates a dense outer crown that can shade interior foliage.</p>\n<h2>Plant growth regulators</h2><p>Plant growth regulators can alter growth, flowering, and fruiting. The course notes utility use for maintaining clearance and describes antigibberellins as reducing cell elongation and growth rates, potentially reducing pruning requirements and water-sprout production after pruning.</p></main>", "/arboriculture-operations/tree-selection-and-planting": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Arboriculture Operations</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Selecting the right tree for the site, evaluating nursery stock, planting correctly, and supporting establishment after transplanting.</p></div>\n<main>\n<div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Right plant, right place</li><li>Nursery stock</li><li>Planting</li><li>Staking and mulch</li><li>Establishment care</li></ul></div><h2>Right plant, right place</h2><p>Selection begins by matching site conditions with plant traits. Consider climate, moisture, exposure, soil pH, texture, volume, salinity, compaction and organic matter, along with utilities, pavement, structures, crown-clearance needs, future development, and maintenance access.</p><p>Plant traits include hardiness, heat tolerance, preferred soil, mature height and spread, root system, pest and disease vulnerability, maintenance needs, and nuisance or ornamental attributes.</p><h2>Planting stock</h2><p>Common stock types include bare-root, balled-and-burlapped, containerized, container-grown, and in-ground fabric bag stock. Each has tradeoffs in cost, handling, planting window, root condition, and size. Inspect the root system and locate the <strong>trunk flare</strong>, the transition from trunk to structural roots. Encircling or girdling roots can impair vascular function and future growth.</p><h2>Planting</h2><p>Moisture availability is a central timing concern. The planting hole should be shallow and wide, with the trunk flare at ground level. The source material recommends retaining on-site backfill soil and a hole approximately twice the root-ball diameter. Avoid routine amendments and fertilization without a soil-based reason.</p><h2>Staking and mulch</h2><p>Most trees do not require staking, but it may be useful on windy sites, for small root systems, poor trunk taper, or large evergreen crowns. Supports should allow some movement and must be removed before they girdle the tree. Mulch is presented at about 2–3 inches deep, extending outward where practical and kept away from the trunk.</p><h2>Establishment</h2><p>Irrigation is the most important establishment treatment. Monitor and adjust for species, exposure, soil type, and drainage. Early pruning should focus on dead, diseased, and broken branches; avoid heavy live-crown removal while the tree is recovering from transplanting.</p>\n\n</main>", "/disorders-and-disturbances/plant-health-care": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Disorders &amp; Disturbances</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Plant Health Care is broader than treating pests. It aims to maintain plant health, prevent problems, monitor change, and use appropriate controls only when needed.</p></div>\n<main><div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Tree health and defense</li><li>PHC and IPM</li><li>Monitoring and action thresholds</li><li>Management goals</li><li>Control strategies</li></ul></div>\n<h2>Tree health and defense</h2><p>The course describes a healthy tree as healthy in appearance, structure, and vitality. <strong>Vitality</strong> is the plant's ability to overcome stress and thrive, while <strong>vigor</strong> is its genetic capacity to resist stress. Arborists are usually concerned with vitality.</p><p>Plant defenses include physical structures and chemical compounds. Management can support defense by matching species to sites, selecting resistant species or cultivars, maintaining landscape diversity, and choosing appropriate timing for practices such as pruning.</p>\n<h2>PHC versus IPM</h2><table class=\"key-table\"><thead><tr><th>Plant Health Care</th><th>Integrated Pest Management</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Holistic and strongly preventive; emphasizes maintaining health and cultural controls.</td><td>Manages pests through multiple controls and reduces reliance on pesticides.</td></tr><tr><td>Treats trees as part of a larger ecosystem.</td><td>Uses monitoring and action thresholds to guide decisions.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2>Monitoring</h2><p>Regular inspections collect information about the site, plant, and disorders and can quantify pest populations or disorder severity. Action thresholds can vary with plant health, phenology, and timing. The course distinguishes <strong>key stressors</strong>—frequently encountered pests, disorders, or site conditions—and <strong>key plants</strong>, which may be species with frequent pest problems or plants of high client value.</p>\n<h2>Management goals</h2><div class=\"concept-grid\"><div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Prevention</h3><p>The preferred approach: promote plant health and prevent problems before they begin.</p></div><div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Suppression</h3><p>Reduce a pest population to a tolerable level.</p></div><div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Eradication</h3><p>Total removal of a pest from an area.</p></div></div>\n<h2>Control strategies</h2><p>PHC and IPM can combine <strong>cultural, mechanical, biological, and chemical controls</strong>. Biological control may involve introduction, conservation, or augmentation of predators, parasites, or pathogens. Chemical controls require attention to nontarget effects, pest resurgence, and pesticide resistance. Alternative pesticide options in the course include insecticidal soaps, horticultural oils, botanical pesticides, insect growth regulators, and microbial pesticides.</p>\n<details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Check: Why isn't “eradicate every pest” the default PHC objective?</summary><div><p>PHC focuses on maintaining plant health and managing pests to tolerable levels. IPM decisions use monitoring and thresholds and seek effective control with minimal harmful effects and disturbance.</p></div></details></main>", "/disorders-and-disturbances/tree-diagnostics": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Disorders &amp; Disturbances</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Good diagnosis is a process of narrowing possibilities. Start with the plant and pattern, examine the whole site and tree, and resist treating a symptom as a diagnosis.</p></div>\n<main><div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Signs and symptoms</li><li>Diagnostic vocabulary</li><li>The six-step diagnostic process</li><li>Root and root-collar clues</li><li>Laboratory sampling</li></ul></div>\n<h2>Signs versus symptoms</h2><p><strong>Signs</strong> are direct indications of a causal agent and can be diagnostic—for example galleries or conks. <strong>Symptoms</strong> are effects produced in the plant, such as wilting or necrosis, and often have multiple possible causes.</p>\n<h3>Describe before you diagnose</h3><p>Useful symptom vocabulary in the course includes blight, blotch, canker, chlorosis, decay, dieback, gall, gummosis, necrosis, oozing, scorch, spot, stippling, stunting, vascular discoloration, wilt, and witch's broom.</p>\n<h2>A six-step diagnostic process</h2><ol class=\"process-list\"><li><strong>Accurately identify the plant.</strong> Many issues are host specific.</li><li><strong>Look for patterns of abnormality.</strong> Compare the plant with what is normal for the species and season; ask whether other plants are affected.</li><li><strong>Carefully examine the site.</strong> Determine whether affected plants are restricted to an area and what has changed on the site.</li><li><strong>Note foliage colour, size, and thickness.</strong> Look for premature colour change, twisting, curling, or abnormal development.</li><li><strong>Check the trunk and branches.</strong> Look for discoloration, drying, oozing, loose bark, wounds, emergence holes, and reduced growth.</li><li><strong>Examine roots and the root collar.</strong> Check trunk flare, constricting roots, root condition, soil moisture, and evidence of anaerobic conditions or disease.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"pull\"><p>Work systematically and rule out possibilities. Trees often have more than one problem, so a “spiral of decline” may be more realistic than a single-cause explanation.</p></div>\n<h2>Root clues</h2><p>The course emphasizes that many problems arise underground. Healthy roots should be evaluated for flexibility, firmness, and colour. Brown roots can be associated with dry soil or toxic chemicals; black roots can indicate anaerobic conditions, disease, or pathogens.</p>\n<h2>Laboratory analyses</h2><p>When symptoms alone are insufficient, sample the affected area and provide the host identity, history, and surrounding site conditions. For suspected pathogens, sample the transition between diseased and healthy tissue. Insects and mites can be placed in a sealed vial, jar, or plastic bag; leaves or branches can be wrapped in dry paper towel and sealed in a plastic bag; mushrooms and fruiting bodies should be kept in dry paper bags or boxes.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/disorders-and-disturbances/diagnostic-practice/\">Try the interactive diagnostic practice →</a></p></main>", "/disorders-and-disturbances/section-quiz": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Disorders &amp; Disturbances</p><p class=\"standfirst\">A cumulative quiz drawing from the lessons in this section. Questions are shuffled on each attempt; answer all questions, then check your score and review the explanations.</p></div>\n<main><section class=\"activity section-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-toolbar\"><p id=\"quizProgress\"></p><button id=\"restartQuiz\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New attempt</button></div><div id=\"sectionQuiz\"></div><div class=\"actions\"><button id=\"gradeQuiz\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button></div><div id=\"quizResult\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></section></main>", "/disorders-and-disturbances/pests-and-pathogens": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Disorders &amp; Disturbances</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Biotic agents are living causes of tree disorders. Understanding the host, causal agent, environment, and pattern of injury helps narrow the diagnosis.</p></div>\n<main><div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Biotic agents</li><li>Pest groups</li><li>Plant diseases</li><li>The disease triangle</li><li>Alternate hosts and invasive species</li></ul></div>\n<h2>Biotic agents</h2><p>Biotic agents are living and include pests and infectious pathogens such as fungi, bacteria, viruses, and related organisms. They are often limited to particular hosts and may begin in isolated parts of a plant.</p>\n<h2>Pests</h2><p>A pest may compete with desirable plants, threaten plant health or structural integrity, diminish appearance, or reduce utility and enjoyment of the landscape. Pest groups highlighted in the course include <strong>borers, leafminers, aphids and scales, mites, nematodes, and other animals</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"concept-grid\"><div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Borers</h3><p>Look for evidence such as emergence holes or galleries as well as the tree's response to disrupted tissues.</p></div><div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Sap-feeding pests</h3><p>Aphids, scales, and mites can produce foliage symptoms, but the organism or other direct evidence is more diagnostic than the symptom alone.</p></div></div>\n<h2>Diseases</h2><p>The lecture introduces blights, vascular wilts, phytoplasmas, and rusts. Disease diagnosis should combine host identity, signs and symptoms, distribution, site conditions, and—when needed—laboratory analysis.</p>\n<h3>The plant disease triangle</h3><p>Disease development is framed through the interaction of the plant, the causal organism, and a conducive environment. A susceptible host and a pathogen do not automatically produce severe disease if environmental conditions do not support disease development.</p>\n<h2>Alternate hosts and invasive species</h2><p>Some disease cycles involve alternate hosts. Invasive pests and pathogens can be especially damaging because host trees may have little resistance and introduced pests may lack effective predators. The course notes quarantine zones as a common management response in Canada and the United States.</p>\n<details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Check: Why is accurate host identification an early diagnostic priority?</summary><div><p>Many biotic problems are host specific. Correct identification can quickly reduce the list of plausible pests and pathogens.</p></div></details></main>", "/disorders-and-disturbances/abiotic-disturbances": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Disorders &amp; Disturbances</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Abiotic disturbances are noninfectious problems caused by nonliving environmental, site, or mechanical factors. Their patterns often provide the first clue to diagnosis.</p></div>\n<main>\n<div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Stress, disorders, and temporality</li><li>Recognizing abiotic patterns</li><li>Mechanical and soil-related injury</li><li>Weather and environmental injury</li><li>Competition, pollution, and chemical injury</li></ul></div>\n<h2>Stress and disturbance</h2><p>A <strong>disorder</strong> is an abnormal condition that impairs plant function. Abiotic agents are nonliving and noninfectious. Disturbances may be <strong>acute</strong>, occurring suddenly with rapid damage, or <strong>chronic</strong>, developing over long periods. Lightning and untimely frost are acute examples; poor drainage and soil compaction are chronic examples.</p>\n<div class=\"concept-grid\"><div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Acute</h3><p>Sudden onset and nearly immediate damage. Think storms, lightning, rapid freeze, or mechanical injury.</p></div><div class=\"concept-card\"><h3>Chronic</h3><p>Slow onset over time. Think compaction, drainage problems, persistent site limitations, or recurring stress.</p></div></div>\n<h2>Look for patterns</h2><p>The course contrasts biotic and abiotic patterns. Abiotic injury can affect multiple genera or families exposed to the same condition and may appear relatively uniformly across a plant or site. Biotic agents are more often host-limited and may begin in isolated portions of a plant.</p>\n<details class=\"knowledge-check\"><summary>Check: Several unrelated tree species along one newly graded strip decline at the same time. Which category should be high on your list?</summary><div><p>An abiotic site disturbance. A shared pattern across unrelated hosts suggests examining grading, drainage, compaction, chemicals, or other site-wide factors before assuming a host-specific pest or pathogen.</p></div></details>\n<h2>Mechanical and soil-related injury</h2><p>Examples in the course include root and trunk damage, soil compaction, nutrient deficiencies, flooding, and girdling roots. These problems can disrupt water and mineral uptake, gas exchange, vascular transport, or structural integrity.</p>\n<h2>Weather and environmental injury</h2><p>Rapid freeze, ice storms, hurricanes, sunscald, lightning, and heat can injure trees directly. Timing and distribution matter: ask what weather event occurred, when symptoms appeared, and whether similarly exposed plants show comparable injury.</p>\n<h2>Other abiotic agents</h2><p>Competition and allelopathy, pollution, and chemical injury are additional noninfectious causes. Diagnosis requires connecting the observed pattern with site history and exposure rather than relying on a single symptom.</p>\n<div class=\"pull\"><p><strong>Diagnostic principle:</strong> symptoms such as scorch, chlorosis, wilt, or dieback describe what the tree is doing; they do not by themselves identify the cause.</p></div>\n</main>", "/disorders-and-disturbances/diagnostic-practice": "<div class=\"page-head\">\n\t<p class=\"standfirst\">\n\t\tPractice matching key terms to their definitions and to photos, and put the steps\n\t\tof diagnosing a tree problem in the right order.\n\t</p>\n</div>\n\n<main>\n\n\t<section class=\"activity\" aria-labelledby=\"terms-heading\">\n\t\t<h2 id=\"terms-heading\">Health and diagnostic terms</h2>\n\t\t<p>Drag each term onto its matching definition, or tap a term and then tap the definition it belongs to.</p>\n\t\t<div id=\"termsMatch\" class=\"match-activity\">\n\t\t\t<div id=\"termsBank\" class=\"term-bank\" aria-label=\"Terms to place\"></div>\n\t\t\t<div id=\"termsList\" class=\"match-list\"></div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"checkTermsButton\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"resetTermsButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New Questions</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div id=\"termsFeedback\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\t</section>\n\n\t<section class=\"activity\" aria-labelledby=\"photos-heading\">\n\t\t<h2 id=\"photos-heading\">Identify signs and symptoms</h2>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tIs this photo a sign (direct evidence of the causal agent) or a symptom (an effect\n\t\t\tof the causal agent)? Choose one &mdash; a correct answer moves you to the next photo.\n\t\t</p>\n\t\t<p id=\"photoQuizProgress\" class=\"photo-progress\"></p>\n\t\t<div class=\"photo-quiz-display\">\n\t\t\t<img id=\"photoQuizImage\" class=\"quiz-photo\" src=\"\" alt=\"Photo of a tree sign or symptom to identify\">\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions photo-choice-actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"signButton\" class=\"choice-button\" type=\"button\">Sign</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"symptomButton\" class=\"choice-button\" type=\"button\">Symptom</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div id=\"photoQuizFeedback\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"restartPhotoQuizButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">Restart</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t</section>\n\n\t<section class=\"activity\" aria-labelledby=\"phc-heading\">\n\t\t<h2 id=\"phc-heading\">PHC and control strategies</h2>\n\t\t<p>Drag each term onto its matching definition, or tap a term and then tap the definition it belongs to.</p>\n\t\t<div id=\"phcMatch\" class=\"match-activity\">\n\t\t\t<div id=\"phcBank\" class=\"term-bank\" aria-label=\"Terms to place\"></div>\n\t\t\t<div id=\"phcList\" class=\"match-list\"></div>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"checkPhcButton\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"resetPhcButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New Questions</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div id=\"phcFeedback\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\t</section>\n\n\t<section class=\"activity\" aria-labelledby=\"steps-heading\">\n\t\t<h2 id=\"steps-heading\">Order the diagnostic steps</h2>\n\t\t<p>\n\t\t\tDrag the steps into the order you should follow when diagnosing a tree problem, from\n\t\t\tfirst to last. On a touchscreen or keyboard, use the up and down buttons on each step\n\t\t\tinstead of dragging.\n\t\t</p>\n\t\t<ol id=\"stepsList\" class=\"steps-list\"></ol>\n\t\t<div class=\"actions\">\n\t\t\t<button id=\"checkStepsButton\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Order</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"revealStepsButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">Show Correct Order</button>\n\t\t\t<button id=\"resetStepsButton\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">Shuffle</button>\n\t\t</div>\n\t\t<div id=\"stepsFeedback\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n\t</section>\n\n\t\n\n</main>", "/urban-forestry/urban-forest-management": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Urban Forestry</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Managing trees as a population: benefits and costs, inventories, canopy, equity, planning, maintenance, ordinances, and wood-waste strategies.</p></div>\n<main>\n<div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Benefits and costs</li><li>Inventories and canopy</li><li>Management plans</li><li>Maintenance and response</li><li>Wood waste</li></ul></div><h2>Why urban forests matter</h2><p>Urban trees can improve air quality, manage stormwater, aid infiltration, reduce erosion, store carbon, provide shade, reduce heat and wind, support wildlife, reduce energy demand, and contribute social, economic, and health benefits. They also carry costs and conflicts: planting and maintenance expense, risk, infrastructure conflicts, pollen and volatile compounds, debris, screening, and invasive-species impacts.</p><h2>Measuring the resource</h2><p><strong>Tree inventories</strong> organize information about individual trees and populations. <strong>Urban tree canopy</strong> describes the area covered by tree crowns when projected onto the ground. Canopy mapping can reveal distribution patterns and support planning, while tree-equity analysis considers whether benefits are distributed fairly across neighborhoods.</p><h2>Plans, standards, and specifications</h2><p>Urban forest management can include planting, preservation, maintenance and operations, removal and replacement, risk management, emergency response, outreach, and community engagement. Specifications translate broader requirements into detailed procedures for work.</p><h2>Maintenance and emergency response</h2><p>Young-tree structural pruning can reduce later defects and maintenance burden. Storm response requires organized priorities, safe operations, and decisions about damaged trees and infrastructure.</p><h2>Wood waste</h2><p>Wood can be chipped, retained as wildlife habitat where appropriate, reclaimed, converted to biochar, or used as biomass fuel. Disposal choices matter because anaerobic decomposition can produce methane and burning releases stored carbon.</p>\n\n</main>", "/urban-forestry/section-quiz": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Urban Forestry</p><p class=\"standfirst\">A cumulative quiz drawing from the lessons in this section. Questions are shuffled on each attempt; answer all questions, then check your score and review the explanations.</p></div>\n<main><section class=\"activity section-quiz\"><div class=\"quiz-toolbar\"><p id=\"quizProgress\"></p><button id=\"restartQuiz\" class=\"secondary\" type=\"button\">New attempt</button></div><div id=\"sectionQuiz\"></div><div class=\"actions\"><button id=\"gradeQuiz\" class=\"primary\" type=\"button\">Check Answers</button></div><div id=\"quizResult\" class=\"feedback\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div></section></main>", "/urban-forestry/trees-and-construction": "<div class=\"page-head\"><p class=\"lesson-kicker\">Urban Forestry</p><p class=\"standfirst\">Protecting retained trees through planning, design, construction, and post-construction care, with attention to roots, soil, grade, and exposure.</p></div>\n<main>\n<div class=\"lesson-nav\"><strong>On this page</strong><ul><li>Construction impacts</li><li>Protection zones</li><li>Root cutting and compaction</li><li>Grade and exposure</li><li>Development phases</li></ul></div><h2>Construction can start a spiral of decline</h2><p>Construction can injure trunks and branches, sever roots, compact soil, change grades, alter drainage and soil chemistry, and suddenly expose formerly sheltered crowns. Effects may appear well after construction is complete.</p><h2>Tree protection zones</h2><p>Terms such as <strong>dripline</strong>, <strong>critical root zone</strong>, and <strong>tree protection zone</strong> help define areas where impacts should be limited. The appropriate protected area depends on tree condition, species tolerance, site constraints, and the work proposed.</p><h2>Root cutting and soil compaction</h2><p>Root cutting can be selective or non-selective, but location, root size, amount removed, tree condition, and species tolerance all affect consequences. Soil compaction reduces pore space and therefore water infiltration, oxygen availability, and root growth. Construction specifications for buildings and roads often demand compaction levels that are hostile to tree roots.</p><h2>Grade changes and exposure</h2><p>Adding fill or cutting soil can alter oxygen, moisture, and rooting conditions. Removing neighboring trees or structures can also expose a retained tree to wind and sunlight conditions for which its existing form did not develop.</p><h2>Development phases</h2><p><strong>Planning</strong> evaluates trees for preservation and considers where structures might go. <strong>Design</strong> creates plans, specifications, and protection zones. <strong>Construction</strong> executes those plans while impacts occur. <strong>Post-construction</strong> care includes monitoring and addressing changed soil and water conditions.</p><div class=\"pull\"><p>Tree preservation works best when arboricultural input occurs before site design is fixed, not after construction has already begun.</p></div>\n\n</main>"} as const;
export const meta = {"/botany/tree-biology": {"title": "Tree Biology", "description": "How trees capture energy, move water, grow, compartmentalize injury, and organize their major tissues and organs."}, "/botany/section-quiz": {"title": "Botany Section Quiz", "description": "Cumulative practice across tree biology and identification."}, "/botany/tree-identification": {"title": "Tree Identification", "description": "A practical framework for taxonomy and recognizing trees by foliage, bark, branching, cones, flowers, fruit, and other morphological traits."}, "/soil-and-nutrition/soil-analysis": {"title": "Soil Analysis", "description": "Arborist practice questions on soil analysis: the soil texture triangle and the pH scale."}, "/soil-and-nutrition/tree-nutrition": {"title": "Tree Nutrition", "description": "Essential elements, nutrient availability, deficiency symptoms, and soil and foliar testing."}, "/soil-and-nutrition/water-management": {"title": "Water Management", "description": "Tree water needs, drought and flooding, irrigation methods, conservation, mulch, and drainage."}, "/soil-and-nutrition/soil-science": {"title": "Soil Science", "description": "How soils form, function, store water and nutrients, and support tree roots in natural and urban environments."}, "/soil-and-nutrition/section-quiz": {"title": "Soils & Nutrition Section Quiz", "description": "Cumulative practice across the Soils &amp; Nutrition domain."}, "/soil-and-nutrition/tree-fertilization": {"title": "Tree Fertilization", "description": "Interactive tree nutrition and fertilization worksheet: read N-P-K labels and calculate fertilizer amounts."}, "/arboriculture-operations/tree-risk-and-support": {"title": "Tree Risk, Support & Lightning Protection", "description": "How tree structure, loads, defects, targets, consequences, mitigation, support systems, and lightning protection fit into tree risk management."}, "/arboriculture-operations/worker-safety-and-climbing": {"title": "Tree Worker Safety & Climbing", "description": "Core jobsite safety concepts, communication, work zones, electrical awareness, climbing terminology, rope systems, and emergency response."}, "/arboriculture-operations/section-quiz": {"title": "Arboriculture Operations Section Quiz", "description": "Cumulative practice across pruning, planting, safety, climbing, and tree risk."}, "/arboriculture-operations/pruning": {"title": "Pruning", "description": "Pruning biology, specifications, cuts, timing, objectives, systems, young-tree training, and special cases."}, "/arboriculture-operations/tree-selection-and-planting": {"title": "Tree Selection, Planting & Establishment", "description": "Selecting the right tree for the site, evaluating nursery stock, planting correctly, and supporting establishment after transplanting."}, "/disorders-and-disturbances/plant-health-care": {"title": "Plant Health Care", "description": "Plant health care, integrated pest management, monitoring, action thresholds, and control strategies."}, "/disorders-and-disturbances/tree-diagnostics": {"title": "Tree Diagnostics", "description": "Use signs, symptoms, patterns, site history, and a systematic examination to diagnose tree problems."}, "/disorders-and-disturbances/section-quiz": {"title": "Disorders & Disturbances Section Quiz", "description": "Cumulative practice across the Disorders &amp; Disturbances domain."}, "/disorders-and-disturbances/pests-and-pathogens": {"title": "Pests & Pathogens", "description": "An introduction to arboricultural pests, pathogens, disease development, and invasive species."}, "/disorders-and-disturbances/abiotic-disturbances": {"title": "Abiotic Disturbances", "description": "Recognize nonliving causes of tree stress and the patterns that help distinguish abiotic from biotic problems."}, "/disorders-and-disturbances/diagnostic-practice": {"title": "Disorders and Diagnostics", "description": "Arborist practice questions about tree disorders, diagnostics, and plant health care."}, "/urban-forestry/urban-forest-management": {"title": "Urban Forest Management", "description": "Managing trees as a population: benefits and costs, inventories, canopy, equity, planning, maintenance, ordinances, and wood-waste strategies."}, "/urban-forestry/section-quiz": {"title": "Urban Forestry Section Quiz", "description": "Cumulative practice across urban forest management and trees during construction."}, "/urban-forestry/trees-and-construction": {"title": "Trees & Construction", "description": "Protecting retained trees through planning, design, construction, and post-construction care, with attention to roots, soil, grade, and exposure."}} as const;
export const quizzes = {"/botany/section-quiz": [{"q": "What is the primary product formed from water and carbon dioxide during photosynthesis?", "o": ["Glucose", "Lignin", "Nitrogen", "ATP only"], "a": 0, "e": "Photosynthesis uses light energy to form glucose from water and carbon dioxide."}, {"q": "What does transpiration describe?", "o": ["Water-vapor loss primarily through stomata", "Sugar movement in phloem", "Cell division in cambium", "Root anchorage"], "a": 0, "e": "Transpiration is water-vapor loss, primarily through leaf stomata."}, {"q": "Which tissue produces phloem outward and xylem inward?", "o": ["Cambium", "Heartwood", "Pith", "Cork only"], "a": 0, "e": "Cambium is meristematic tissue responsible for secondary growth."}, {"q": "Primary growth primarily increases a tree's:", "o": ["Length", "Girth", "Heartwood proportion", "Bark thickness only"], "a": 0, "e": "Primary growth occurs at growing points and increases length."}, {"q": "What is a mycorrhiza?", "o": ["A symbiotic association between fungi and absorbing roots", "A type of bark crack", "A leaf margin", "A conifer cone"], "a": 0, "e": "Mycorrhizae are symbiotic associations involving fungi and absorbing roots."}, {"q": "Which CODIT wall separates new growth from previously injured tissue?", "o": ["Wall 1", "Wall 2", "Wall 3", "Wall 4"], "a": 3, "e": "Wall 4 is the barrier zone formed after injury."}, {"q": "Why are palms anatomically different from typical woody trees?", "o": ["They lack a typical vascular cambium and secondary growth", "They lack xylem and phloem", "They have no apical meristem", "They are gymnosperms"], "a": 0, "e": "Palms are monocots with vascular bundles and no typical vascular cambium."}, {"q": "In a scientific name, Pinus in Pinus ponderosa is the:", "o": ["Genus", "Family", "Specific epithet", "Order"], "a": 0, "e": "The first word is the genus; ponderosa is the specific epithet."}, {"q": "Which foliage description best matches many pines?", "o": ["Needles in fascicles", "Flat single needles with upright cones", "Sharp single needles on woody pegs", "Scale leaves only"], "a": 0, "e": "Pine needles are commonly grouped in fascicles."}, {"q": "Which is useful for broadleaf identification?", "o": ["Leaf margin and arrangement", "Only tree height", "Only geographic location", "Only bark color"], "a": 0, "e": "Identification uses multiple morphological traits, including leaf margin and arrangement."}, {"q": "What does respiration do in a tree?", "o": ["Moves water from roots to leaves", "Converts light energy into glucose", "Produces new xylem cells", "Releases usable energy from carbohydrates using oxygen"], "a": 3, "e": "Respiration breaks down carbohydrates with oxygen to release energy for growth, defense, transport, and storage."}, {"q": "Glucose that is not used immediately is commonly stored as:", "o": ["Calcium", "Nitrate", "Starch", "Chlorophyll"], "a": 2, "e": "Glucose can be used right away, stored as starch, or converted into cellulose, lipids, amino acids, and other compounds."}, {"q": "Which part of the stem contains functional xylem?", "o": ["Heartwood", "Branch bark ridge", "Outer bark", "Sapwood"], "a": 3, "e": "Sapwood contains functional xylem; heartwood is older inner wood."}, {"q": "Osmosis moves water across a semipermeable membrane toward:", "o": ["Higher water potential", "Higher oxygen concentration", "Lower water potential", "Cooler tissue"], "a": 2, "e": "Water moves from higher water potential toward lower water potential."}, {"q": "Earlywood and latewood reflect:", "o": ["The difference between sapwood and heartwood", "Primary versus secondary growth", "Healthy versus decayed wood", "Different portions of the annual growth cycle"], "a": 3, "e": "Both form during secondary growth at different times within one growing season."}, {"q": "Which CODIT wall resists vertical spread of decay?", "o": ["Wall 2", "Wall 4", "Wall 3", "Wall 1"], "a": 3, "e": "Wall 1 resists vertical spread, Wall 2 inward, Wall 3 lateral, and Wall 4 is the barrier zone formed after injury."}, {"q": "Which set of conditions can restrict photosynthesis?", "o": ["Mycorrhizae, healthy roots, and mild temperatures", "Full sun, adequate water, and good soil oxygen", "Shade, drought, and nutrient deficiency", "Large leaves, high chlorophyll content, and open stomata"], "a": 2, "e": "Shade, drought, temperature extremes, low chlorophyll, poor soil oxygen, and nutrient deficiency can all limit photosynthesis."}, {"q": "Which taxonomic rank is the most specific?", "o": ["Genus", "Order", "Species", "Family"], "a": 2, "e": "The hierarchy runs domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species, becoming more specific at each step."}, {"q": "Single, stiff, sharp needles attached on woody pegs with hanging cones suggest:", "o": ["Spruce (Picea)", "Pine (Pinus)", "Fir (Abies)", "Oak (Quercus)"], "a": 0, "e": "Spruce needles sit on woody pegs and its cones hang down."}, {"q": "Single, flat, blunt needles with upright cones suggest:", "o": ["Pine (Pinus)", "Maple (Acer)", "Fir (Abies)", "Spruce (Picea)"], "a": 2, "e": "Fir foliage is generally flat and blunt, and fir cones stand upright on the branch."}, {"q": "Why are scientific names preferred over common names?", "o": ["Common names are not allowed in reports", "Scientific names describe the tree's size", "Scientific names change every year", "Common names vary, while scientific names give a consistent reference"], "a": 3, "e": "One species can have several common names, and one common name can refer to different species."}, {"q": "Tree identification is most reliable when:", "o": ["Only bark is examined", "The common name is known", "One distinctive leaf feature is found", "Several independent traits point to the same taxon"], "a": 3, "e": "Combining foliage, bark, branching, flowers, fruit, and form reduces misidentification."}], "/soil-and-nutrition/section-quiz": [{"q": "Which soil horizon is rich in organic matter and contains many absorbing roots?", "o": ["A horizon", "B horizon", "C horizon", "R horizon"], "a": 0, "e": "The A horizon contains many absorbing roots and is rich in soil organic matter."}, {"q": "Which soil particle class has the greatest surface area?", "o": ["Sand", "Silt", "Clay", "Gravel"], "a": 2, "e": "Clay particles are the smallest and have the greatest surface area."}, {"q": "High bulk density is most directly associated with which condition?", "o": ["High porosity", "Low porosity", "High macropore volume", "Rapid infiltration"], "a": 1, "e": "High bulk density corresponds with reduced pore space."}, {"q": "What does cation exchange capacity describe?", "o": ["Soil resistance to erosion", "Ability to retain and exchange positively charged ions", "Rate of water infiltration", "Amount of oxygen in soil"], "a": 1, "e": "CEC is the soil’s capacity to attract, retain, and exchange cations."}, {"q": "Which soils generally have greater buffering capacity?", "o": ["Soils high in clay and organic matter", "Coarse sands with little organic matter", "Only saline soils", "Only compacted soils"], "a": 0, "e": "Clay and soil organic matter contribute to greater buffering capacity."}, {"q": "In a clay-over-sand profile, why can water accumulate in the clay?", "o": ["The sand is impermeable", "A capillary barrier delays movement into the larger pores below", "Clay always repels water", "Sand has higher CEC"], "a": 1, "e": "A textural boundary can create a capillary barrier until the upper layer becomes sufficiently wet."}, {"q": "A tree wilts on hot afternoons but recovers overnight. This most closely matches:", "o": ["Temporary drought stress", "Chronic flooding", "Nitrogen toxicity", "Salt crusting"], "a": 0, "e": "Temporary wilting can occur when transpiration temporarily exceeds water uptake."}, {"q": "Flooded roots are primarily challenged by:", "o": ["Excess oxygen", "Lack of gas exchange", "Excess photosynthesis", "Low humidity"], "a": 1, "e": "Flooding restricts air and gas exchange and can cause root death."}, {"q": "Which irrigation principle is emphasized in the course?", "o": ["Apply water faster than infiltration", "Match application to infiltration and plant needs", "Always irrigate shallowly", "Ignore precipitation inputs"], "a": 1, "e": "Irrigation should reflect plant needs, soil conditions, infiltration, and precipitation."}, {"q": "Which three are primary macronutrients on fertilizer labels?", "o": ["N, P, K", "Ca, Mg, S", "C, H, O", "Fe, Mn, Zn"], "a": 0, "e": "Nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium are the primary macronutrients."}, {"q": "Why can a nutrient be present in soil yet unavailable to a tree?", "o": ["Availability can be limited by pH, compaction, waterlogging, or other site conditions", "Soil tests measure only atmospheric nutrients", "Roots never absorb mineral elements", "All nutrients are equally available at every pH"], "a": 0, "e": "Presence in soil does not guarantee plant availability or uptake."}, {"q": "What does a foliar test measure in this course?", "o": ["Soil texture", "Nutrient amount in foliage", "Soil bulk density", "Irrigation rate"], "a": 1, "e": "Foliar testing evaluates nutrients in foliage."}, {"q": "Which fertilizer application places nutrients directly in the root zone?", "o": ["Subsurface application", "Wound dressing", "Crown reduction", "Foliar pruning"], "a": 0, "e": "Subsurface dry or liquid fertilizer is placed into the root zone."}, {"q": "Why should fertilizer use be based on analysis?", "o": ["To identify deficiencies and consider pH and site conditions", "Because all trees need annual fertilizer", "To maximize salt accumulation", "Because foliage symptoms are always diagnostic"], "a": 0, "e": "The course recommends soil and, when appropriate, foliar analyses before addressing deficiencies."}, {"q": "A 50 lb fertilizer bag labeled 16-4-8 contains how many pounds of nitrogen?", "o": ["4 lb", "8 lb", "16 lb", "32 lb"], "a": 1, "e": "16% of 50 lb is 8 lb."}, {"q": "Which is NOT one of the five soil-forming factors?", "o": ["Time", "Parent material", "Cation exchange capacity", "Relief"], "a": 2, "e": "The five factors are climate, organisms, relief, parent material, and time. CEC is a soil property, not a formation factor."}, {"q": "Which soil horizon is a zone of accumulation?", "o": ["A horizon", "E horizon", "O horizon", "B horizon"], "a": 3, "e": "Materials leached from upper horizons accumulate in the B horizon."}, {"q": "Macropores in soil mainly:", "o": ["Promote aeration and drainage", "Hold water against gravity", "Bind clay particles together", "Increase bulk density"], "a": 0, "e": "Macropores are too large to retain much water against gravity, so they drain and fill with air. Micropores hold water."}, {"q": "A soil with pH 5 is how much more acidic than a soil with pH 6?", "o": ["100 times", "10 times", "2 times", "1 time (no difference)"], "a": 1, "e": "The pH scale is logarithmic, so each whole unit is a tenfold change."}, {"q": "Which pH range does the course identify as favourable for most plants?", "o": ["About 4.0–4.5", "About 8.5–9.0", "About 7.5–8.0", "About 6.0–6.5"], "a": 3, "e": "Slightly acidic soil around 6.0–6.5 favours nutrient availability for most plants."}, {"q": "Which amendment is used to raise soil pH?", "o": ["Sulfur", "Aluminum sulfate", "Lime", "Peat moss"], "a": 2, "e": "Lime raises pH and sulfur lowers it, although effects may be limited in space and time."}, {"q": "Sodic soils are high in which element?", "o": ["Nitrogen", "Calcium", "Sulfur", "Sodium"], "a": 3, "e": "Sodic soils can develop crusting, high pH, nutrient imbalances, and poor structure."}, {"q": "The soil moisture reservoir is smallest for:", "o": ["A small-rooted tree in sandy soil", "A large-rooted tree in clay soil", "A large-rooted tree in loam", "A small-rooted tree in clay soil"], "a": 0, "e": "The reservoir is the rooted soil volume times its water-holding capacity, so small root systems and sandy soils hold the least."}, {"q": "Evapotranspiration combines:", "o": ["Plant transpiration and evaporation from the soil", "Infiltration and runoff", "Photosynthesis and respiration", "Rainfall and irrigation"], "a": 0, "e": "It is the total water leaving a site through plants and the soil surface."}, {"q": "Drain tiles help wet sites by removing:", "o": ["Gravitational water", "Water from the tree's xylem", "Soluble salts only", "Water held in micropores"], "a": 0, "e": "Drain tiles remove gravitational water, so they can't fix heavy clay or overwatering on their own."}, {"q": "Grouping plants into hydrozones means:", "o": ["Planting only along drainage ditches", "Watering every zone on the same schedule", "Installing one drip line per tree", "Grouping plants by similar water needs"], "a": 3, "e": "Hydrozones let irrigation match each group's needs and conserve water."}, {"q": "Which are the secondary macronutrients?", "o": ["Iron, manganese, and zinc", "Carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen", "Sulfur, magnesium, and calcium", "Nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium"], "a": 2, "e": "N, P, and K are the primary macronutrients; S, Mg, and Ca are secondary."}, {"q": "Nitrogen-deficiency chlorosis is first observed on:", "o": ["Flowers", "Older leaves", "Newest leaves only", "Roots"], "a": 1, "e": "Nitrogen is mobile in the plant, so chlorosis appears first on older leaves and can later spread to young ones."}, {"q": "A 25 lb bag labeled 30-12-10 contains how many pounds of nitrogen?", "o": ["3 lb", "30 lb", "7.5 lb", "2.5 lb"], "a": 2, "e": "The first number is percent nitrogen: 25 × 0.30 = 7.5 lb."}, {"q": "Where should a newly planted tree be irrigated?", "o": ["Only on the soil beyond the planting hole", "At the edge of the eventual mature canopy", "Only at the trunk", "Around the periphery of the root ball"], "a": 3, "e": "Roots are initially concentrated in the root ball, so water should reach it and the soil just around it."}], "/disorders-and-disturbances/section-quiz": [{"q": "Which best defines an abiotic agent?", "o": ["A living infectious agent", "A nonliving, noninfectious agent", "Any fungal pathogen", "Any insect pest"], "a": 1, "e": "Abiotic agents are nonliving and noninfectious."}, {"q": "Which is an example of an acute disturbance from the course?", "o": ["Long-term compaction", "Poor drainage", "Lightning", "Chronic nutrient limitation"], "a": 2, "e": "Lightning occurs suddenly and can cause immediate damage."}, {"q": "A problem affects several unrelated species uniformly across one site. What should be strongly considered?", "o": ["An abiotic site factor", "A host-specific pathogen only", "A single borer species only", "Normal phenology only"], "a": 0, "e": "Shared injury across unrelated hosts is a useful clue for an abiotic cause."}, {"q": "Which is a sign rather than a symptom?", "o": ["Wilting", "Necrosis", "A fungal conk", "Chlorosis"], "a": 2, "e": "A conk is direct evidence of a causal organism and therefore a sign."}, {"q": "What is the first step in the diagnostic sequence taught in the course?", "o": ["Apply a pesticide", "Identify the plant accurately", "Collect a soil sample", "Prune symptomatic branches"], "a": 1, "e": "Accurate plant identification narrows host-specific possibilities."}, {"q": "After identifying the plant, what should the diagnostician examine next?", "o": ["Patterns of abnormality", "Fertilizer label", "Only the roots", "Only nearby insects"], "a": 0, "e": "The course next asks the arborist to look for patterns of abnormality."}, {"q": "Which root colour is associated in the course with anaerobic conditions, disease, or pathogens?", "o": ["Black", "Bright white only", "Blue", "Orange"], "a": 0, "e": "Black roots are listed as a clue for anaerobic conditions, disease, or pathogens."}, {"q": "For suspected pathogens, where should a diagnostic sample be taken?", "o": ["Only completely healthy tissue", "The transition between diseased and healthy tissue", "Only dead bark far from symptoms", "Any random leaf"], "a": 1, "e": "Sampling the interface captures affected and adjacent tissue useful for diagnosis."}, {"q": "What is the primary emphasis of Plant Health Care?", "o": ["Maintaining health and preventing problems", "Eradicating every organism", "Routine pesticide use", "Treating symptoms without diagnosis"], "a": 0, "e": "PHC is holistic and preventive, with a strong emphasis on cultural practices."}, {"q": "IPM decisions are based in part on:", "o": ["Monitoring and action thresholds", "Automatic calendar spraying", "Tree height alone", "Fertilizer grade alone"], "a": 0, "e": "Monitoring and action thresholds are core IPM principles."}, {"q": "What does suppression mean in PHC goals?", "o": ["Total removal of a pest from an area", "Reducing a pest population to a tolerable level", "Ignoring the pest", "Increasing pest abundance"], "a": 1, "e": "Suppression reduces the pest population to a tolerable level."}, {"q": "Which is a form of biological control described in the course?", "o": ["Conservation of existing predators", "Flush cutting", "Soil compaction", "Topping"], "a": 0, "e": "Conservation biological control retains existing natural enemies."}, {"q": "Why can invasive species be especially damaging?", "o": ["Hosts may lack resistance and pests may lack predators", "They are always abiotic", "They affect only one leaf", "They cannot spread through trade"], "a": 0, "e": "Novel host-pest relationships can lack evolved resistance and effective predators."}, {"q": "Which statement best distinguishes symptoms from signs?", "o": ["Symptoms are effects on the plant; signs directly indicate the causal agent", "Symptoms are always diagnostic; signs never are", "Only insects produce signs", "Abiotic problems never produce symptoms"], "a": 0, "e": "Symptoms are plant responses; signs are direct evidence of the causal agent."}, {"q": "Why should diagnosis be systematic?", "o": ["Trees may have multiple interacting problems and similar symptoms can have different causes", "Every symptom has one cause", "Site history is irrelevant", "Root examination is unnecessary"], "a": 0, "e": "A framework helps avoid missed clues and accounts for multiple interacting stresses."}, {"q": "In the course, vitality describes a tree's:", "o": ["Genetic capacity to resist stress", "Age in years", "Ability to overcome stress and thrive", "Total height and spread"], "a": 2, "e": "Vigor is genetic capacity; vitality is how well the tree is actually coping, which is usually what arborists assess."}, {"q": "In PHC, key plants may be:", "o": ["Plants that never need monitoring", "Only native species", "Species with frequent pest problems or plants of high client value", "Any tree over 30 years old"], "a": 2, "e": "Key plants and key stressors help focus monitoring effort."}, {"q": "What does eradication mean as a PHC goal?", "o": ["Total removal of a pest from an area", "Reducing a pest to a tolerable level", "Preventing problems before they start", "Monitoring without action"], "a": 0, "e": "Eradication is complete removal, which is rarely the default goal."}, {"q": "The plant disease triangle consists of:", "o": ["Prevention, suppression, and eradication", "Susceptible host, pathogen, and conducive environment", "Signs, symptoms, and samples", "Roots, trunk, and crown"], "a": 1, "e": "Disease develops when all three interact; without a conducive environment, severe disease may not occur."}, {"q": "A susceptible host and a pathogen are present, but the weather is unfavourable for infection. What is most likely?", "o": ["Severe disease may not develop", "The host becomes resistant permanently", "The pathogen becomes abiotic", "Severe disease is guaranteed"], "a": 0, "e": "The environment is one side of the disease triangle; unfavourable conditions can limit disease."}, {"q": "Brown roots are associated in the course with:", "o": ["Dry soil or toxic chemicals", "Healthy, active growth", "Excess mycorrhizae", "Normal winter dormancy"], "a": 0, "e": "Black roots suggest anaerobic conditions or pathogens; brown roots can indicate dry soil or chemical injury."}, {"q": "How should mushrooms or fruiting bodies be sent to a lab?", "o": ["Sealed in a wet plastic bag", "Frozen in foil", "In dry paper bags or boxes", "Submerged in water"], "a": 2, "e": "Paper keeps fruiting bodies from sweating and rotting in transit."}, {"q": "How should insects and mites be packaged for lab analysis?", "o": ["Loose in a paper envelope", "Pressed in a book", "In a sealed vial, jar, or plastic bag", "Mixed into a soil sample"], "a": 2, "e": "Sealed containers keep specimens intact and contained."}, {"q": "Which pattern more often suggests a biotic agent?", "o": ["Uniform damage across many unrelated species", "Damage appearing right after a hard freeze on all plants", "Injury along a recently graded strip", "Damage limited to one host species, starting in isolated parts of the plant"], "a": 3, "e": "Biotic agents are frequently host-specific and localized; abiotic injury tends to cross species and look uniform."}, {"q": "Which is an example of a chronic disturbance?", "o": ["Lightning strike", "Soil compaction", "Untimely frost", "Ice storm"], "a": 1, "e": "Chronic disturbances develop slowly over long periods; the others are acute."}, {"q": "Releasing additional natural enemies into a landscape to boost an existing population is:", "o": ["Augmentation", "Conservation", "Eradication", "Cultural control"], "a": 0, "e": "Biological control can involve introduction, conservation, or augmentation of natural enemies."}, {"q": "Which is listed in the course as an alternative pesticide option?", "o": ["Flush cuts", "Horticultural oils", "Soil compaction", "Topping"], "a": 1, "e": "Alternatives include insecticidal soaps, horticultural oils, botanical pesticides, insect growth regulators, and microbial pesticides."}, {"q": "Which concern applies specifically to chemical controls?", "o": ["Girdling roots", "Loss of branch collars", "Increased soil bulk density", "Pest resurgence and pesticide resistance"], "a": 3, "e": "Chemical controls also require attention to nontarget effects."}, {"q": "Why might a 'spiral of decline' be more realistic than a single-cause diagnosis?", "o": ["Abiotic problems never affect trees", "Trees can only have one problem at a time", "Trees often have multiple interacting problems", "Pathogens always act alone"], "a": 2, "e": "Stress from one factor can make a tree vulnerable to others, so causes compound."}, {"q": "What is a common management response to invasive pests in Canada and the United States?", "o": ["Adding fill soil", "Topping affected trees", "Quarantine zones", "Increasing irrigation"], "a": 2, "e": "Quarantines limit movement of infested material to slow spread."}], "/arboriculture-operations/section-quiz": [{"q": "When planting, where should the trunk flare generally finish?", "o": ["At ground level", "30 cm below grade", "At the bottom of the root ball", "Covered by mulch"], "a": 0, "e": "The source material places the trunk flare at ground level."}, {"q": "What is emphasized as the most important aspect of tree establishment?", "o": ["Irrigation", "Fertilization", "Crown reduction", "Trunk wrapping"], "a": 0, "e": "Irrigation is identified as the most important establishment treatment."}, {"q": "A job briefing should identify:", "o": ["Tasks, hazards, controls, PPE, and communication", "Only the crew leader", "Only equipment fuel levels", "Only the client address"], "a": 0, "e": "Job briefings coordinate work and establish hazard controls and communication."}, {"q": "What is a drop zone?", "o": ["Area where cut material is expected to fall or be lowered", "Vehicle parking only", "The entire property", "A refueling location"], "a": 0, "e": "The drop or landing zone must be clear before cutting."}, {"q": "In an MRS climbing system:", "o": ["The climbing line moves through the tie-in point", "The rope is always stationary", "No tie-in point is used", "Only ladders are used"], "a": 0, "e": "In a moving rope system, the line moves through the tie-in point as the climber moves."}, {"q": "Tree-part failure occurs when:", "o": ["Load exceeds strength", "Strength exceeds load", "Wind stops", "The tree has heartwood"], "a": 0, "e": "The structural concept presented is strength < load = failure."}, {"q": "Which factor is essential to tree risk in addition to likelihood of failure?", "o": ["Targets and consequences", "Leaf color only", "Tree age only", "Species name only"], "a": 0, "e": "Risk assessment considers likelihood, targets, impact, and consequences."}, {"q": "Which assessment level uses specialized equipment or detailed analysis for a specific concern?", "o": ["Advanced", "Limited visual", "Basic only", "Screening only"], "a": 0, "e": "Advanced assessment provides detailed information using specialized tools, data, or expertise."}, {"q": "Which support system generally permits tree movement?", "o": ["Dynamic cabling", "Rigid bracing only", "Static cabling", "Concrete fill"], "a": 0, "e": "Dynamic cable systems are described as allowing tree movement."}, {"q": "Early pruning of a newly planted tree should generally prioritize:", "o": ["Dead, diseased, and broken branches", "Heavy crown thinning", "Removing all low branches", "Topping"], "a": 0, "e": "Early establishment pruning should be conservative to preserve photosynthetic capacity."}, {"q": "Which pruning specification provides vertical clearance?", "o": ["Reduce", "Thin", "Clean", "Raise"], "a": 3, "e": "Raising removes lower branches for clearance; clean removes dead/diseased/broken, thin reduces density, reduce shrinks height or spread."}, {"q": "Where is a branch removal cut made?", "o": ["At any internode", "Just outside the branch collar and branch bark ridge", "Midway along the branch", "Flush with the trunk"], "a": 1, "e": "Cutting outside the collar and ridge preserves the tissues that help the tree compartmentalize."}, {"q": "Why are flush cuts inappropriate?", "o": ["They leave too long a stub", "They remove protective tissues at the branch base", "They only work on conifers", "They are too slow to make"], "a": 1, "e": "Flush cuts remove the branch collar and branch protection zone."}, {"q": "A reduction cut should be made to a lateral at least what fraction of the removed branch's diameter?", "o": ["One-half", "One-third", "Twice", "One-tenth"], "a": 1, "e": "A lateral smaller than one-third makes it a heading cut."}, {"q": "Under the referenced guidance, no more than how much mature-tree foliage should be removed at once?", "o": ["All lower foliage", "One-half", "Two-thirds", "One-quarter"], "a": 3, "e": "Remove only what is needed to meet the objective; mature trees tolerate less than young ones."}, {"q": "When should last-season bloomers be pruned to preserve next year's flowers?", "o": ["In late winter", "Just before bloom", "Just after bloom", "Any time in autumn"], "a": 2, "e": "Their buds form on the previous season's growth, so pruning soon after bloom avoids removing them."}, {"q": "What is lion tailing?", "o": ["Removing dead wood only", "Removing lower and interior foliage, leaving growth at branch ends", "Pruning palms to the bud", "Training a dominant leader"], "a": 1, "e": "Lion tailing is malpractice because it shifts weight outward and removes interior foliage."}, {"q": "How does pollarding differ from topping?", "o": ["They are the same practice", "Topping preserves pollard heads", "Pollarding is done once on mature trees", "Pollarding repeatedly removes sprouts back to established heads; topping makes large heading cuts"], "a": 3, "e": "Topping is linked to poor compartmentalization, weak regrowth, and higher failure potential."}, {"q": "Which palm pruning practice is generally recommended?", "o": ["Use climbing spurs on the trunk", "Remove all fronds annually", "Make flush cuts into the trunk", "Avoid removing green fronds"], "a": 3, "e": "Palm wounds don't close over, so remove only dead, hazardous, or loose material."}, {"q": "Which pruning tool blade type is preferred?", "o": ["Anvil", "Blunt cleaver", "Serrated anvil", "Bypass"], "a": 3, "e": "Bypass blades cut cleanly rather than crushing tissue."}, {"q": "What does the three-cut method prevent?", "o": ["Bark tearing or splitting when removing a heavy limb", "Compaction under the drip line", "Sprouting after pruning", "Pest transmission"], "a": 0, "e": "An undercut and a relief cut remove the limb's weight before the final cut."}, {"q": "In rope terminology, a bend:", "o": ["Joins two rope ends", "Is tied in a single rope only", "Measures a rope's working-load limit", "Secures a rope to an object"], "a": 0, "e": "A knot is tied in one rope, a hitch secures a rope to an object or rope, and a bend joins ropes."}, {"q": "In a stationary rope system (SRS):", "o": ["The climber uses spurs instead of rope", "The climber moves along a stationary line", "No anchor is needed", "The line moves through the tie-in point"], "a": 1, "e": "In an MRS, by contrast, the line moves through the tie-in as the climber moves."}, {"q": "How should electrical conductors be treated on a job site?", "o": ["As potentially carrying fatal voltage", "As safe in dry weather", "As safe unless sparking", "As safe if insulated"], "a": 0, "e": "Assume every conductor is energized and lethal."}, {"q": "A fuel spill on equipment should be handled by:", "o": ["Cleaning it before starting the engine", "Starting the engine to evaporate it", "Covering it with sawdust and working", "Ignoring it if small"], "a": 0, "e": "Fuel must also be stored in approved containers away from ropes."}, {"q": "Why should tree stakes allow some movement and be removed on time?", "o": ["Stakes make roots grow deeper", "Staking replaces irrigation", "Movement prevents all pests", "Movement builds trunk taper, and supports left on can girdle the tree"], "a": 3, "e": "Most trees don't need staking; when used, it should be temporary and flexible."}, {"q": "Wind most commonly produces which type of load on a tree?", "o": ["Chemical", "Thermal", "Static", "Dynamic"], "a": 3, "e": "Gravity is a common static force; wind loads change constantly."}, {"q": "Which tree risk assessment level is used for broad screening of many trees?", "o": ["Root collar excavation", "Limited visual", "Advanced", "Basic"], "a": 1, "e": "Basic is a detailed visual inspection of one tree and site; advanced uses specialized tools or analysis."}, {"q": "What is residual risk?", "o": ["Risk before any assessment", "Risk from roots only", "Risk that is always zero after pruning", "Risk that remains after mitigation"], "a": 3, "e": "All trees carry some risk; mitigation reduces but rarely eliminates it."}, {"q": "A lightning protection system is intended to:", "o": ["Replace cabling", "Strengthen the trunk mechanically", "Provide a preferred conductive path to ground", "Prevent lightning from striking the area"], "a": 2, "e": "It needs specialized design, installation, and inspection."}], "/urban-forestry/section-quiz": [{"q": "Which is an environmental benefit of urban trees?", "o": ["Stormwater management", "Guaranteed elimination of all risk", "Increased soil compaction", "Removal of all maintenance costs"], "a": 0, "e": "Urban trees can help manage stormwater and aid infiltration."}, {"q": "What does urban tree canopy describe?", "o": ["Area covered by tree crowns projected onto the ground", "Only public trees", "Only forest reserves", "Total trunk area"], "a": 0, "e": "Canopy cover is based on crown area projected onto the ground."}, {"q": "A tree inventory is primarily used to:", "o": ["Organize information about individual trees and populations", "Measure only rainfall", "Replace all field inspections", "Determine property ownership"], "a": 0, "e": "Inventories support population-level management and planning."}, {"q": "Which may be part of an urban forest management plan?", "o": ["Planting, preservation, maintenance, risk, and emergency response", "Only tree removal", "Only nursery purchasing", "Only pest spraying"], "a": 0, "e": "Management planning can span the full lifecycle and community program."}, {"q": "Which is a possible beneficial use of wood waste?", "o": ["Wood chips or reclaimed wood", "Dumping into waterways", "Burning in all situations", "Burying anaerobically"], "a": 0, "e": "The material discusses chips, reclaimed wood, habitat, biochar, and biomass uses."}, {"q": "Why can soil compaction harm retained trees?", "o": ["It reduces pore space, infiltration, oxygen, and root growth", "It always increases soil oxygen", "It increases rooting volume", "It prevents all evaporation"], "a": 0, "e": "Compaction reduces the pore environment roots need."}, {"q": "When is tree preservation most effectively integrated into development?", "o": ["During planning, before design is fixed", "Only after construction", "Only after root cutting", "At final inspection only"], "a": 0, "e": "Early planning allows tree needs to influence structure placement and site design."}, {"q": "What is a tree protection zone intended to do?", "o": ["Limit damaging activity around retained trees", "Increase vehicle access under crowns", "Provide a soil stockpile area", "Mark only the trunk diameter"], "a": 0, "e": "Protection zones establish areas where construction impacts should be limited."}, {"q": "A sudden removal of neighboring shelter can create what issue for a retained tree?", "o": ["Changed exposure to wind and sunlight", "Immediate improved anchorage", "Guaranteed lower transpiration", "No meaningful change"], "a": 0, "e": "Construction can alter exposure conditions a tree did not develop under."}, {"q": "Which phase creates plans, specifications, and protection zones?", "o": ["Design", "Post-construction", "Demolition only", "Emergency response"], "a": 0, "e": "The design phase documents how construction activity and tree protection will be controlled."}, {"q": "Tree-equity analysis asks whether:", "o": ["Every tree is the same species", "All neighborhoods have identical soils", "Tree benefits are distributed fairly across neighborhoods", "Trees cost the same to maintain"], "a": 2, "e": "Canopy mapping can reveal neighborhoods with fewer trees and fewer benefits."}, {"q": "Why do wood-waste disposal choices matter for the climate?", "o": ["Anaerobic decomposition can produce methane and burning releases stored carbon", "Wood chips are always toxic", "Burning wood removes carbon from the air", "Biochar cannot be made from urban wood"], "a": 0, "e": "Reuse options include chips, habitat, reclaimed lumber, biochar, and biomass fuel."}, {"q": "What do specifications do in urban forest management?", "o": ["Count every tree in the inventory", "Translate broad requirements into detailed work procedures", "Set the city's annual budget", "Replace the need for a management plan"], "a": 1, "e": "Plans set direction; standards and specifications define how work is done."}, {"q": "Which is a cost or conflict associated with urban trees?", "o": ["Carbon storage", "Stormwater management", "Infrastructure conflicts", "Reduced energy demand"], "a": 2, "e": "Costs also include maintenance, risk, debris, pollen, and invasive-species impacts."}, {"q": "Why is young-tree structural pruning valuable for a city?", "o": ["It eliminates all tree risk", "It replaces watering", "It can reduce later defects and maintenance burden", "It is required only after storms"], "a": 2, "e": "Early, small cuts prevent larger structural problems later."}, {"q": "When might construction damage to a retained tree become visible?", "o": ["Only before construction starts", "Only during the first week", "Never, if the tree survives a year", "Well after construction is complete"], "a": 3, "e": "Decline can lag for years, which makes cause and effect easy to miss."}, {"q": "Adding fill soil over a tree's root zone can harm it mainly by:", "o": ["Altering oxygen, moisture, and rooting conditions", "Removing bark", "Adding too many nutrients", "Increasing sunlight on leaves"], "a": 0, "e": "Fill can smother roots; cutting grade can sever them."}, {"q": "What happens in the post-construction phase?", "o": ["Deciding where buildings will go", "Monitoring and addressing changed soil and water conditions", "Drafting tree protection plans", "Excavating foundations"], "a": 1, "e": "Care continues after the work ends because impacts can appear later."}, {"q": "The appropriate size of a tree protection area depends on:", "o": ["Only the property line", "Only the trunk diameter", "Only the contractor's schedule", "Tree condition, species tolerance, site constraints, and the work proposed"], "a": 3, "e": "Dripline and critical root zone are starting points, adjusted for the tree and site."}, {"q": "Why can construction compaction specifications be a problem for trees?", "o": ["They require too much mulch", "They mandate root pruning", "Compaction levels required for buildings and roads are hostile to roots", "They prohibit irrigation"], "a": 2, "e": "Engineering compaction reduces pore space, infiltration, and oxygen."}, {"q": "The consequences of root cutting depend on:", "o": ["Only the tool used", "Nothing; all root cuts are equal", "Location, root size, amount removed, tree condition, and species tolerance", "Only the time of day"], "a": 2, "e": "Selective cuts far from the trunk on a tolerant, healthy tree are least damaging."}, {"q": "Structural cells are designed to:", "o": ["Replace tree protection fencing", "Carry pavement loads while keeping spaces of uncompacted soil for roots", "Drain all water from the root zone", "Compact soil for roads"], "a": 1, "e": "They support root growth under pavement but are costly. Structural soils are an alternative."}]} as const;
